window.__SYSTEM_DESIGN_ENGLISH__={"request-lifecycle":{"summary":"Follow one page request from the moment you press Enter until the page appears.","goals":["Learn how DNS finds a server and why its answers are cached.","See why opening a connection takes round trips.","Follow a request from browser to server to database and back.","See where page-load time is spent.","Compare a slow database query with a user far from the server."],"steps":[{"title":"A page feels slow","body":["To make a page faster, first find out where it spends time. Follow one request for a shopping page.","You type <code>shop.example</code> and press Enter. The page appears about 155 milliseconds later. Several computers have talked to each other along the way."],"callout":"A web request is a conversation between computers. Each message takes time, so the delays add up."},{"title":"Find the server’s address (DNS)","body":["People use names such as <code>shop.example</code>. Networks send data to an <span class=\"term\">IP address</span>, a number that identifies a computer on the network. The browser asks a <span class=\"term\">DNS resolver</span> to find the address for the name.","The resolver returns <code>203.0.113.7</code> in about 20 milliseconds."],"callout":"A <span class=\"term\">time to live (TTL)</span> says how long the browser may reuse a DNS answer. While the answer is still valid, a repeat visit can skip this lookup."},{"title":"Open a secure connection","body":["The browser connects to <code>203.0.113.7</code>. A <span class=\"term\">TCP handshake</span> sets up the connection. A <span class=\"term\">TLS handshake</span> sets up encryption so other people on the network cannot read the traffic.","Each handshake needs a <span class=\"term\">round trip</span>: a message goes to the server and an answer comes back. Here one round trip takes 30 milliseconds, so the two handshakes take about 60 milliseconds."],"callout":"Round-trip time (RTT) mostly depends on the network distance and route between the computers. A faster server cannot remove that travel time."},{"title":"Send the page request","body":["The browser sends <code>GET /products</code> over the open connection. <code>GET</code> asks the server to return the product page.","The server reads the request, checks the user’s session, and runs the page code. This takes one more round trip plus about 10 milliseconds of server work."],"changes":["+ Request: 1 round trip + 10 ms of server work"]},{"title":"The server reads the database","body":["The product information is stored in a <span class=\"term\">database</span>, not in the web server. The server asks the database for the product rows, then waits for the answer.","Watch the request travel to the database and the data travel back."],"callout":"A <span class=\"term\">stateless server</span> does not keep a user’s session only in its own memory between requests. Keeping shared data in a database lets any web server handle the next request, which helps when we add more servers."},{"title":"Send the page back","body":["The server uses the database rows to build HTML and sends it to the browser. Downloading it takes about 20 milliseconds. The browser shows the page 155 milliseconds after you pressed Enter.","The browser then sees that the page needs images and style files. It sends more requests. Reusing the open connection means these requests do not need the handshakes again."],"changes":["+ Download: 20 ms","~ Page appears after 155 ms"]},{"title":"Load the page’s other files","body":["The HTML page also points to images, style sheets, scripts, and fonts—about 40 more files. The browser has to fetch those files too.","With HTTP/1.1, each connection usually handles one request at a time, so a browser opens several connections and queues the rest. <span class=\"term\">HTTP/2</span> can carry many requests at once over one connection. This is called <span class=\"term\">multiplexing</span>."],"callout":"Smaller files are quicker to send. Sites can bundle scripts, compress text, use modern image formats, and load below-the-fold content only when needed."},{"title":"Case: a slow database query","body":["The shop adds a “recommended for you” box. For every page view, its database query sorts all products by a score. Now the database takes 400 milliseconds to answer.","The network and connection steps have not changed, but the page now takes more than half a second. Requests wait in line for the database."],"callout":"Measure before changing code. In this case the network is fine; the slow query is the problem. The Indexes lesson shows one way to speed up some queries."},{"title":"Case: the user is far away","body":["Return to the fast query, but open the page in Sydney while the server is in Virginia. Each round trip now takes about 200 milliseconds instead of 30.","The two connection handshakes plus the page request add about 600 milliseconds before the page downloads. The server and code are unchanged, but the page takes more than four times as long."],"callout":"Reduce round trips by reusing connections and using newer network protocols. A <span class=\"term\">content delivery network (CDN)</span> stores copies of files on servers closer to users. Placing services in more regions can also reduce distance."},{"title":"Recap: where the time went","body":["Of the 155 milliseconds, the server and database spend only about 25 milliseconds doing work. Roughly 130 milliseconds is time spent waiting for messages to cross the network.","<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li><b>DNS</b> finds an IP address for a name, and its answer can be cached.</li><li>Opening connections takes <b>round trips</b>; reuse connections when you can.</li><li>Server code may be a small part of the delay, unless a <b>slow query</b> holds things up.</li><li>Greater <b>distance</b> increases the time for every round trip.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: Latency & Throughput explains what happens to these delays when many users arrive at once."}]},"latency-throughput":{"summary":"Learn two ways to measure speed, and why busy servers can become slow before reaching their limit.","goals":["Tell the time for one request apart from the number completed each second.","See why response time rises quickly when a server is almost full.","Understand p50 and p99 response times.","See why network distance adds a lower limit to response time.","Learn how work in progress, fan-out, and batching affect a system."],"steps":[{"title":"Two ways to measure speed","body":["<span class=\"term\">Latency</span> is how long one request takes to get an answer. Watch one dot travel to the server and back.","<span class=\"term\">Throughput</span> is how many requests the system completes each second. Count the dots."],"callout":"Think of a highway: latency is how long one trip takes; throughput is how many cars pass in an hour. More lanes can move more cars, but they do not make one car’s trip faster when the road is empty."},{"title":"More users, same server","body":["Traffic grows seven times. Throughput also rises, so the server is still keeping up. It is now busy 70% of the time: it handles 350 requests each second out of a capacity of about 500.","Some requests now wait in line before the server can work on them. That wait is part of latency."],"callout":"<span class=\"term\">Utilization</span> is the share of time a server is busy. At 70% utilization, a new request will often arrive while the server is already working."},{"title":"Latency rises sharply near the limit","body":["Traffic grows by another third, and the server is now busy 96% of the time. It handles 480 requests per second, just below its limit of 500. Throughput rises a little, but the queue grows quickly and the slowest requests take seven times longer.","Requests do not arrive at perfectly even intervals. When the server has spare capacity, it can catch up after a burst. With only 4% spare, new requests keep joining a line that is already long."],"callout":"Latency rises slowly, then can jump near full utilization. A common target is to keep the server around 60–70% busy at peak and leave the rest as headroom for bursts."},{"title":"The average hides slow requests","body":["At 70% utilization, most requests are quick, but a few wait behind slower work. The average is about 180 milliseconds, which hides how long those slower requests take.","We also measure <span class=\"term\">percentiles</span>. <b>p50</b> is the time that half of requests meet or beat. <b>p99</b> is the time that 99 out of 100 requests meet or beat; about 1 in 100 takes longer."],"callout":"A page can make 30 requests. If each has a 1% chance of being slow, about 26% of page loads will include at least one slow request. The slow end of the range matters to users."},{"title":"Add capacity to reduce waiting","body":["Double the server’s cores. The same 350 requests per second now use about 35% of its capacity. The queue drains, and both p50 and p99 latency fall.","Throughput stays the same because demand has not changed. The extra capacity gives the server room to handle bursts."],"callout":"To reduce latency under load, lower utilization by adding capacity or doing less work for each request."},{"title":"Distance adds latency","body":["Move the server across an ocean. The server itself is just as fast, but the messages have farther to travel, so every round trip takes longer.","Light in fiber travels about 200 kilometers per millisecond. New York and London are about 5,600 kilometers apart, so one round trip cannot be faster than about 56 milliseconds. In practice it is closer to 75 milliseconds."],"callout":"No server upgrade can beat the travel time. To reduce this delay, place a copy of the data closer to users with caches, CDNs, or servers in more regions."},{"title":"Little’s Law: count the work in progress","body":["<span class=\"term\">Little’s Law</span> links throughput, latency, and the number of requests being handled: <b>requests in flight = throughput × latency</b>. At 350 requests per second and 0.12 seconds each, about 42 requests are in flight at once.","Each in-flight request uses resources such as a thread, connection, or memory. If latency grows to one second while throughput stays at 350 requests per second, about 350 requests are in flight. A pool sized for 50 can run out."],"callout":"Use this relationship to estimate thread, connection, and worker capacity. It also shows why a slow service can use up resources while requests wait."},{"title":"One request waits for many servers","body":["A search page asks 100 index servers at the same time and waits for the slowest answer. Each server is fast 99% of the time, but with 100 servers there is a high chance that at least one is slow.","About 63% of searches wait for a server’s p99 response: 1 − 0.99¹⁰⁰ ≈ 63%. A delay that is rare for one server can be common for the whole page."],"callout":"A <span class=\"term\">hedged request</span> sends a second copy to a replica if the first server has not answered by a chosen time, such as its p95. Use whichever answer arrives first."},{"title":"Batch work to increase throughput","body":["Writing analytics events one row at a time lets the database insert about 2,000 rows per second. Much of the cost is the overhead of starting each write.","Instead, collect up to 100 events for at most 50 milliseconds and write them together. Throughput rises to about 50,000 rows per second, but each event may wait up to 50 milliseconds before the write."],"callout":"Batching trades a little more latency for higher throughput. It works well for logs, analytics, and bulk imports, but not for an action where a user is waiting for an immediate answer."},{"title":"Recap: measure both kinds of speed","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li><b>Latency</b> is time per request. <b>Throughput</b> is requests completed per second.</li><li>Latency can jump as utilization nears 100%; keep <b>headroom</b>.</li><li>Watch <b>p99</b> as well as the average, because users notice the slow requests.</li><li><b>Distance</b> adds travel time that a faster server cannot remove.</li><li><b>Requests in flight = throughput × latency</b>. A request that fans out can make rare slow responses common.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: Napkin Math shows how to make rough capacity estimates before building a system."}]},"napkin-math":{"summary":"Use quick estimates to turn a product idea into rough needs for servers, storage, and network capacity.","goals":["Turn daily users and actions into requests per second.","Estimate peak traffic instead of planning only for the average.","Estimate the number of servers and amount of storage.","Estimate network traffic and cache memory.","Remember a few useful time and capacity estimates."],"steps":[{"title":"Start with the size of the problem","body":["You are designing PhotoShare. Before choosing technology, estimate the size of the job: could one server handle it, or might you need many?","The answer does not need to be exact. You need the right <span class=\"term\">order of magnitude</span>—for example, tens of servers rather than one or ten thousand. Napkin math gives you a quick estimate."],"callout":"Engineers and interviewers start with rough numbers, then choose a design that can handle them."},{"title":"Turn daily activity into requests per second","body":["Start with users and how often they use the app. If 10 million users each make 20 requests, that is 200 million requests per day.","A day has 86,400 seconds, so the average is about 2,300 requests per second. For faster mental math, round a day to 100,000 seconds; that gives a rough estimate of 2,000 requests per second."],"callout":"A quick conversion: one million requests per day is about 12 requests per second."},{"title":"Plan for the busy hour","body":["Traffic is not flat all day. More people may use the app in the evening or during a big event. A common rough estimate is that the peak is two to three times the daily average.","Using the more precise average of about 2,300 requests per second, a 3× peak is about 7,000 requests per second. At that load, one server is clearly not enough."],"callout":"A system sized only for its average load may fail when most users want it at the same time."},{"title":"Estimate the number of servers","body":["A load test shows that one server can handle about 1,000 requests per second. The peak is about 7,000, so the basic estimate is seven servers.","Add room for bursts and for a server failure. Plan for about 10 servers. This estimate uses a measured server capacity, not a guess."],"callout":"A <span class=\"term\">load balancer</span> sends requests across these servers. See Module 02 for how it works."},{"title":"Estimate storage growth","body":["Photos take most of the space. If users upload one million photos a day and each is 2 MB, that is about 2 TB of new data each day. Over a year, that is about 730 TB.","If the system keeps three copies of the data so it can survive a disk failure, plan for about 2 PB in the first year. That is far too much for one database server."],"callout":"Stored data keeps growing. Daily request traffic rises and falls, but yesterday’s photos still need space."},{"title":"Estimate network traffic","body":["Uploads are the smaller part: 2 TB per day is about 20 MB per second coming in. Views send much more data out. If each user views 100 photos per day and each thumbnail is 200 KB, that is 200 TB per day going out.","Divide by about 100,000 seconds and the average is roughly 2 GB per second, or 16 Gbit/s. Peak traffic will be higher. Ten web servers should not have to send all those images themselves; a CDN can serve copies closer to users."],"callout":"For apps with lots of images or video, outgoing network traffic can become a limit before the CPUs do. Estimate bytes sent, not just requests."},{"title":"Estimate the cache size","body":["A feed often reads profile details and photo information again and again. Could a cache keep the most-used data in memory? Ten million profiles at 2 KB each use about 20 GB. One million new photo records at 1 KB each use about 1 GB.","That is about 21 GB. It can fit in one cache server’s memory, with a second server as a backup. The estimate does not require an unusual design."],"callout":"A rough estimate can show that a problem is smaller than it first seemed. That is useful too."},{"title":"Keep a few useful numbers in mind","body":["A few approximate times help compare choices. These are ballpark figures; real hardware and networks vary.","<table class=\"compare numbers\"><tbody><tr><td>Read from memory</td><td>about 100 ns</td></tr><tr><td>Read from SSD</td><td>about 100 µs</td></tr><tr><td>Round trip within one data center</td><td>about 0.5 ms</td></tr><tr><td>Round trip across an ocean</td><td>about 75–150 ms</td></tr><tr><td>Seconds in a day</td><td>about 100,000</td></tr></tbody></table>"],"callout":"Remember the scale of each number, not every digit. That is enough for a useful first estimate."},{"title":"Recap: estimate before you build","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li><b>Users × actions ÷ 100,000 seconds</b> gives a rough average requests-per-second estimate.</li><li>Multiply by <b>2–3</b> for a rough peak, then plan for that peak.</li><li><b>Servers = peak traffic ÷ measured capacity per server</b>, plus spare capacity.</li><li><b>Storage = items per day × size per item × 365 × number of copies.</b></li><li>Estimate <b>network bytes</b> and <b>memory</b> too. They can shape the design.</li></ul>"],"callout":"That finishes Foundations. Next: Vertical vs Horizontal Scaling compares a bigger machine with more machines."}]},"vertical-horizontal":{"summary":"When one server gets overloaded, compare making it bigger with adding more servers.","goals":["Recognize the signs that a server is overloaded.","Learn when scaling up helps and where it stops helping.","Learn how scaling out adds capacity with more servers.","See how autoscaling follows regular demand and why sudden spikes need planning.","Understand why more servers need a load balancer."],"steps":[{"title":"Begin with one server","body":["The app runs on one computer. Every request—opening a page or posting a photo—travels from the user’s device to that server and back.","With 100 users, the server has plenty of room. Its CPU is busy only about 1% of the time."],"callout":"A server has limits for CPU, memory, disk, and network. Its <span class=\"term\">capacity</span> is how much work it can do before one of those resources becomes a limit."},{"title":"A sudden jump in traffic","body":["A post becomes popular and traffic grows 120 times. This server can handle about 400 requests each second, but 480 arrive. Its CPU reaches 100%, requests wait in a queue, responses get slow, and some requests time out.","The app needs more capacity. There are two main ways to add it."],"callout":"Overload often shows up as CPU near 100%, a growing queue, slower responses, and then timeouts."},{"title":"Way 1: scale up","body":["Move the app to a bigger computer with more CPU cores, more memory, or faster disks. The app design stays the same, so the code usually needs no changes.","This is <span class=\"term\">vertical scaling</span>, also called <em>scaling up</em>."],"callout":"Scaling up is often a simple first step: it needs no code changes and adds no new kind of component to operate. A machine with four times the capacity may cost about four times as much."},{"title":"Scaling up has a limit","body":["Vertical scaling has a hardware limit. A workload that uses 16 cores well may not use 192 cores as well, because those cores share locks, memory, and a disk. Moving to a bigger machine can require a migration and restart, causing downtime. Large machines can also cost much more, and twice as many cores may not deliver twice as much throughput.","Moving to a new machine can require a restart and a migration, which may cause downtime."],"callout":"Vertical scaling has a hardware limit. Large machines can also cost much more, and twice as many cores may not deliver twice as much throughput."},{"title":"One server is one point of failure","body":["All users depend on this one machine. A bad software release, a full disk, or a power failure can take the whole app offline.","A component whose failure can stop the entire system is a <span class=\"term\">single point of failure</span> (SPOF)."],"callout":"Even the biggest machine can fail. If everything depends on it, one failure can cause a full outage."},{"title":"Way 2: scale out","body":["Run several medium-sized computers side by side. In this example, four servers each have 96 vCPUs and run the same app. If more capacity is needed, add another server. Medium-sized machines can offer good value, and there is no single-machine ceiling.","This is <span class=\"term\">horizontal scaling</span>, also called <em>scaling out</em>. Here, four servers provide twice the capacity of the largest single machine. If one fails, the other three can carry the load."],"callout":"Four medium servers can offer more total capacity than the largest single server. If one fails, the remaining servers may still handle some traffic."},{"title":"Capacity sits unused overnight","body":["Traffic changes through the day. At 8 p.m. the app needs all four servers. At 4 a.m. traffic is one tenth as high, and the servers are only 5% busy.","You still pay for peak capacity even when most of it is idle. Consumer apps often see a 3–10× difference between daily peak and quiet hours."],"callout":"Consumer apps often have daily traffic peaks several times larger than their quiet hours."},{"title":"Let autoscaling follow demand","body":["Because the servers can do the same work, an <span class=\"term\">autoscaler</span> can add or remove them based on demand. For example, it can try to keep average CPU use near 50%.","At night it scales in to two servers, keeping one extra in case of a failure. In the evening it scales back out. This reduces the time you pay for unused machines."],"callout":"Autoscaling works when servers can take requests interchangeably and a load balancer sends traffic to them. The next lessons cover these ideas."},{"title":"Case: a flash sale","body":["Two servers are handling traffic comfortably. A flash sale starts, and traffic grows five times in two minutes. The autoscaler starts new servers, but they take about three minutes to start and warm up.","During those minutes, the two existing servers can handle only about two-thirds of the requests. For a planned sale, add capacity before it starts. Keep spare capacity for surprise spikes."],"callout":"Autoscaling reacts to demand after it appears. For known launches, sales, or broadcasts, start extra servers early."},{"title":"Who sends each user to a server?","body":["Suppose each group of users is set up to contact one server directly. If Server B fails, the quarter of users assigned to it see errors—even though three healthy servers remain.","A client does not know which servers are healthy or busy. A component in front of the servers must choose where each request goes."],"callout":"That component is a <b>load balancer</b>. It sends requests to healthy servers; the next lesson explains how."},{"title":"Recap: scale up or scale out","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Scale up</th><th>Scale out</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>How</td><td>Bigger machine</td><td>More machines</td></tr><tr><td>Code changes</td><td>None</td><td>Some<sup>1</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Ceiling</td><td>Hardware limit</td><td>Practically none</td></tr><tr><td>Failure</td><td>Total outage</td><td>Partial<sup>2</sup></td></tr><tr><td>To grow</td><td>Migrate + restart</td><td>Add a server</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>Premium at the top</td><td>Linear</td></tr></tbody></table>","Scaling out also lets you <b>autoscale</b>: add servers for the evening peak and remove them at night.","<small><sup>1</sup> Servers must be interchangeable — see <em>Stateless Services</em>. <sup>2</sup> A load balancer can route around failed servers.</small>"],"callout":"A common approach is to scale up while it is simple and affordable, then scale out when one machine is no longer enough."}]},"load-balancing":{"summary":"Give a group of servers one address, then decide which server handles each request.","goals":["Why a group of servers needs one front door","Round robin vs. least connections","How health checks route around a failed server","How to avoid a single point of failure in the load balancer","How layer 7 routing and gradual deploys work"],"steps":[{"callout":"Adding servers helps only when requests are spread across them."},{"callout":"The servers behind a load balancer are its <b>pool</b> (also called a target group)."},{"body":["How does the load balancer (LB) choose a server? The simplest rule is <span class=\"term\">round robin</span>: A, then B, then C, then A again. It is simple and shares requests evenly when servers have similar power and requests take about the same amount of work."],"callout":"<b>Weighted round robin</b> sends a larger share to stronger servers. For example, A : B : C = 2 : 1 : 1."},{"callout":"Round robin counts requests. It does not know how much work each request takes."},{"callout":"Other choices include <b>least response time</b>, <b>IP hash</b> (send the same client to the same server), and <b>power of two choices</b> (sample two servers and pick the less busy one)."},{"callout":"A <b>single point of failure</b> (SPOF) is one component whose failure can stop the service. Health checks help avoid a single failed server becoming one."},{"body":["We removed one single point of failure and created another. Every request now passes through one load balancer. If it fails, users cannot reach the healthy servers behind it."],"callout":"Any component every request depends on needs a backup."},{"callout":"Cloud load balancers, such as AWS ELB and Google Cloud Load Balancing, run across multiple machines and zones for you."},{"body":["The active load balancer fails. The standby notices that heartbeats stopped, takes over the virtual IP, and starts forwarding traffic. Clients keep using the same address. A few requests already in progress may need to be retried."],"callout":"This is <b>active–passive failover</b>: a standby takes over when the active component fails. The same pattern can protect databases, caches, and queues."},{"body":["There are two kinds of load balancer. A <b>layer 4</b> balancer sees IP addresses and ports, then forwards connections. A <b>layer 7</b> balancer can read the HTTP request. It can route by URL path: <code>/api/*</code> to API servers and <code>/images/*</code> to an image service. Each group can be scaled and deployed separately."],"callout":"Layer 7 balancers can also route by host name, header, or cookie. They can handle TLS encryption, caching, or compression."},{"body":["Time to release version 2. A <span class=\"term\">rolling deploy</span> updates one server at a time. First, the load balancer <b>drains</b> that server: it sends no new requests there, but lets requests already running finish. The server is updated, checked for health, and added back. Here C has version 2, B is draining, and A is waiting. Users should not see an error."],"callout":"With a <b>blue-green deploy</b>, bring up a complete version 2 pool, switch traffic to it, and switch back if something goes wrong."},{"body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>A load balancer gives a server pool <b>one stable address</b>.</li><li><b>Round robin</b> is simple; <b>least connections</b> adapts when requests take different amounts of time.</li><li><b>Health checks</b> remove failed servers from the pool.</li><li>The load balancer also needs redundancy: use <b>active–passive failover</b> with a shared virtual IP.</li><li><b>Layer 7</b> balancers can route by path; <b>draining</b> helps deploy without interrupting requests.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Stateless Services</b> — what happens when a user’s next request reaches a different server."}]},"stateless":{"summary":"Make any server able to handle the next request by storing user state outside the server.","goals":["What “state” means and why it ties a user to one server","Sticky sessions and what happens when a server fails","How a shared session store lets any server handle a user","How a signed token can carry identity","Why uploaded files belong in object storage"],"steps":[{"body":["Alice signs in and adds two items to her cart. Her request reaches Server A, which keeps a <span class=\"term\">session</span> in memory: information about her login and cart. Her browser stores a cookie with the session ID, which later requests use to find that session."],"callout":"<b>State</b> is information a server remembers between requests, such as a login, a cart, or an unfinished upload."},{"callout":"When one server keeps a user’s state only in its own memory, the other servers cannot use that state."},{"callout":"Sticky sessions keep a user on one server. If many users stick to the same server, it can be overloaded while others sit idle."},{"callout":"With sticky sessions, each server becomes a single point of failure for the users assigned to it."},{"body":["Keep sessions in a shared, fast <span class=\"term\">session store</span>, often Redis. Every server can read Alice’s session from this store, so any server can handle her request. The application servers are now <span class=\"term\">stateless</span>: they do not keep user information between requests. The load balancer can send requests to any server, and Alice stays signed in."],"callout":"The shared store adds about one millisecond to a request. In return, you can add, remove, or lose application servers more easily."},{"callout":"Stateless servers can be replaced without losing a user’s session. This also makes it easier to add or remove servers as traffic changes."},{"body":["Another option is to put some state in each request as a signed <span class=\"term\">token</span>. For example, a JSON Web Token (JWT) can identify Alice. Any server can check the token’s signature without looking up a session. This avoids a shared-store lookup, but a token is hard to cancel before it expires and travels with every request, so keep it small."],"callout":"A common approach is to use tokens for identity and a shared store for larger or quickly changing data, such as carts or revoked logins."},{"callout":"Anything saved only on one server’s disk—uploads, generated PDFs, or temporary files—is unavailable to other servers. It is also lost when that server is replaced."},{"body":["Store files in shared <span class=\"term\">object storage</span>, such as Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage, or Azure Blob Storage. It is designed to store durable files that every server can access. Server A saves the avatar there, and B and C can read the same file. A browser can also fetch images through a CDN."],"callout":"Rule of thumb: servers should not keep information that must survive a request. Put sessions in a shared store or token, files in object storage, and application data in a database."},{"body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>State kept only in a server’s memory <b>ties the user to that server</b>.</li><li><b>Sticky sessions</b> hide the problem until that server fails.</li><li>Move state to a <b>shared store</b> or carry some of it in a <b>signed token</b>.</li><li>Store files in <b>object storage</b>, not on one server’s local disk.</li><li><b>Stateless</b> servers can be added, removed, or replaced more easily.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Caching</b> — the database used by these stateless servers can become a bottleneck."}]},"caching":{"summary":"Keep frequently requested answers in faster storage so the database does less repeated work.","goals":["Why repeated reads can overload a database","How the cache-aside pattern works","Why the cache hit rate matters","How to handle stale data, expiration, and eviction","How to prevent stampedes and repeated misses"],"steps":[{"callout":"Databases can be hard to scale because they hold the source data. A cache can reduce work that does not need to be repeated."},{"callout":"Many systems have a few very popular items and many items that are rarely read."},{"body":["Add an in-memory <span class=\"term\">cache</span>, such as Redis or Memcached. The server checks the cache first. A <b>hit</b> returns the saved answer in about a millisecond. On a <b>miss</b> (amber), the server reads from the database and saves a copy in the cache for next time. This is the <span class=\"term\">cache-aside</span> pattern."],"callout":"A cache reads an answer from memory, without running the original database query. It can often be 10–100 times faster than that query."},{"body":["The <span class=\"term\">hit rate</span> is the share of reads answered by the cache. A miss takes much longer, so a small change in hit rate can matter. In this example, increasing hits from 80% to 95% cuts average read time by more than half and database load by three-quarters."],"callout":"Cache the right data, provide enough memory, and keep entries long enough for them to be reused."},{"callout":"A cache is a second copy of the data. Keeping that copy current is one of the main challenges of caching."},{"body":["When data changes, the server writes it to the database and deletes the cached copy. The next read misses and loads the new value. As a backup, each cache entry has a <span class=\"term\">TTL</span> (time to live): stale data disappears when the timer runs out. Why delete instead of overwriting the cache? Two writes can race, and the older value could arrive last. Deleting is safer because the next read fetches the latest database value."],"callout":"Choose a TTL based on how long stale data is acceptable: perhaps seconds for prices and stock, or hours for a product description."},{"body":["A cache cannot hold everything because memory costs money. When it fills up, it must remove entries to make room. A common rule is <span class=\"term\">LRU</span> (least recently used): remove the entry that has gone the longest without being read. A common alternative is <span class=\"term\">LFU</span> (least frequently used): remove the entry that has been read the fewest times. LFU can help when a short burst of unusual reads would push out the usual popular items."],"callout":"The cache may need to hold only the most popular data. For example, 10% of the data might serve 90% of reads."},{"body":["The home page is the hottest cache key, with 2,000 reads per second. Its TTL expires. Now all those requests miss and ask the database for the same answer. The database was at 8% load; the rush of identical queries makes it stall. This is a <span class=\"term\">cache stampede</span>, also called a thundering herd."],"callout":"A very popular key is most likely to cause a rush of requests when its cache entry expires."},{"body":["Use <span class=\"term\">request coalescing</span>: the first request after a miss fetches the answer, while the others wait briefly and share that result. Also refresh popular keys shortly before they expire. Add random <b>jitter</b> to TTLs so many entries do not expire at the same moment."],"callout":"Some libraries call request coalescing “single-flight.” CDNs use a similar approach called request collapsing."},{"body":["A crawler—or an attacker—requests product IDs that do not exist: <code>/product/99900017</code>, then <code>…18</code> and <code>…19</code>. Each request misses the cache and asks the database, which finds no row. Because the cache stores no answer, every request is a miss, even if repeated. This is called <span class=\"term\">cache penetration</span>."],"callout":"A cache-aside system can also remember “not found.” If it does not, the database must repeat the same unsuccessful lookup."},{"body":["First, use <span class=\"term\">negative caching</span>: store “not found” for a short time, so repeated requests for the same missing ID stop at the cache. A crawler may request a new ID each time. For that, keep a <span class=\"term\">Bloom filter</span> of valid IDs in server memory. This compact structure can say “definitely not present” or “possibly present.” Reject impossible IDs without asking the cache or database."],"callout":"A Bloom filter does not mistakenly reject an item that exists, but it can say “possibly present” for an item that is missing. Here, a 1% false-positive rate uses about 10 bits per key."},{"callout":"The closer a cache is to the user, the faster it can answer. But nearby copies can be harder to update when the source data changes."},{"body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Cache answers that are <b>read often and change rarely</b>.</li><li><b>Cache-aside</b>: check the cache, read from the database on a miss, then fill the cache.</li><li>The <b>hit rate</b> affects speed and database load.</li><li>Plan for stale data: <b>delete cached copies after writes</b>, set a <b>TTL</b>, and make room with <b>LRU</b>.</li><li>Protect popular keys from <b>stampedes</b> by sharing one fetch and refreshing before expiry.</li><li>Cache <b>“not found”</b> results and use a <b>Bloom filter</b> to screen impossible IDs.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Content Delivery</b> — cache files on servers around the world, closer to users."}]},"cdn":{"summary":"Serve shared files from servers near users to reduce distance and protect the origin.","goals":["Why users far from a server experience more delay","How edge servers cache static files","How cold caches and versioned names affect updates","How a CDN helps protect the origin","When to keep personal pages out of a CDN cache"],"steps":[{"callout":"Light takes time to travel. To reduce distance-related delay, serve content from a location closer to the user."},{"callout":"Images, scripts, stylesheets, and videos are often static files: the same for many users and changed infrequently."},{"body":["A <span class=\"term\">CDN</span> (content delivery network) has <span class=\"term\">edge servers</span> in many cities. Point static files to the CDN, and users can get them from a nearby edge. London now gets images in about 10 ms. Only cache misses (amber) need to travel back to the origin in Virginia."],"callout":"Common CDNs include Cloudflare, Fastly, Akamai, and Amazon CloudFront. You use their edge servers instead of running your own."},{"callout":"A CDN edge is a <b>pull-through cache</b>: it fetches a file from the origin when first asked, saves a copy, and serves later requests from that copy."},{"callout":"A long TTL (time to live) keeps files in the CDN longer and makes it faster. But users may keep seeing an old version until the cache expires or is cleared."},{"body":["Put a fingerprint of the file contents in its name, such as <code>logo.3f9a.png</code>. A changed logo gets a different name, so the CDN cannot mistake it for the old file. Now the file can be cached for a <b>year</b>. The HTML can use a short TTL or skip caching, and point to the latest file names."],"callout":"Build tools such as Vite and webpack can add these content fingerprints to file names."},{"callout":"A CDN is a cache spread across many locations. It can serve traffic spikes at the edge and reduce the load on your origin."},{"body":["A configuration change tells the CDN to cache every page for five minutes. Bob opens his cart, and the edge saves it. Alice opens her cart a second later, and the edge sends her Bob’s cart. Pages that depend on the user must be marked <code>Cache-Control: private</code> (or <code>no-store</code>), so the CDN does not serve one person’s page to another."],"callout":"Treat personal pages as private by default. Cache public content only when you intend to."},{"body":["A CDN can have hundreds of edge servers. If a new file becomes popular, every edge may miss and request the same file from the origin at once. An <span class=\"term\">origin shield</span> is a cache between the edge servers and the origin. Edges ask the shield; the shield asks the origin, reducing duplicate fetches."],"callout":"Many CDNs offer an origin shield setting. Choose one near your origin."},{"body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Distance adds delay; <b>edge servers</b> place content closer to users.</li><li>CDNs are <b>pull-through caches</b>: an edge fetches files from the origin when it misses.</li><li><b>Versioned file names</b> let you cache for a long time while still publishing new versions.</li><li>Edges can absorb traffic spikes and reduce load on the origin.</li><li>Do not publicly cache <b>personal pages</b>; an <b>origin shield</b> can reduce duplicate fetches.</li></ul>"],"callout":"That wraps up Scaling. Next: <b>Data & Storage</b>, starting with SQL vs NoSQL."}]},"sql-nosql":{"summary":"Compare relational tables, documents, and key–value databases, and learn when each shape fits.","goals":["How tables, joins, and transactions work","Why relational databases can become harder to scale","How document and key–value stores organize data","How to choose a database for each kind of data","When a specialist search engine can help"],"steps":[{"title":"Store related data in tables","body":["Many apps start with a <span class=\"term\">relational database</span>, often called a SQL database, such as PostgreSQL or MySQL. It stores data in tables with named columns and a defined <span class=\"term\">schema</span>—the rules for what fields each row can contain. Tables can refer to one another using keys. A <b>join</b> combines related rows, such as each order with the customer’s name."],"callout":"SQL databases have mature tools and support. If you are unsure, a relational database is often a safe starting point."},{"title":"Keep related changes together","body":["Moving $50 from Ana’s account to Ben’s takes two updates: subtract $50 from Ana, then add $50 to Ben. If the server crashes between them, the money could disappear. A <span class=\"term\">transaction</span> groups both updates so they both happen or neither happens. Relational databases provide this with the <b>ACID</b> properties, which help protect payments, orders, and inventory."],"callout":"ACID means <b>Atomic</b> (all or nothing), <b>Consistent</b> (rules stay true), <b>Isolated</b> (unfinished changes are hidden), and <b>Durable</b> (a confirmed change is saved)."},{"title":"As one table grows","body":["Now the product catalog goes into the same database. Shoes have sizes, laptops have CPUs, and books have authors. One products table may need hundreds of columns, many empty for most products. Adding a new product type can require a <span class=\"term\">migration</span>: a change to the database structure. On a table with 400 million rows, some migrations can rewrite or lock the table for hours. All writes still go through one database server."],"callout":"At large scale, two common pressure points are changing a rigid table structure and relying on one server for all writes."},{"title":"Keep each product in a document","body":["A <span class=\"term\">document store</span>, such as MongoDB, saves each record as a self-contained document. A shoe and a laptop can have different fields, so adding a product type may not require a database migration. The trade-off is that application code must handle all the different document shapes it has saved. Since one product’s details stay together, reading it can take one fetch. Documents can also be spread across multiple servers."],"callout":"Document stores fit data you usually read or write as one whole object, such as a product, profile, or article."},{"title":"Look up a value by key","body":["A <span class=\"term\">key–value store</span>, such as Redis or DynamoDB, maps each key to a value. It has no joins and little or no query language: the app gets or sets a value by key, often in about a millisecond. Since each key can stand alone, the data is relatively easy to split across many servers."],"callout":"Key–value stores fit sessions, carts, feature flags, or counters that the app fetches by one ID."},{"title":"Understand the trade-offs","body":["Some NoSQL databases are easy to scale out partly because they offer fewer built-in features: joins across records, transactions across many records, or strict consistency may be limited. <table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>SQL</th><th>NoSQL</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Data shape</td><td>Fixed tables</td><td>Often flexible</td></tr><tr><td>Joins</td><td>Built in</td><td>Often handled by your code</td></tr><tr><td>Transactions</td><td>Can cover many rows</td><td>Often limited to one record</td></tr><tr><td>Scaling writes</td><td>Can be harder</td><td>Often built in</td></tr></tbody></table>These are common patterns, not rules. PostgreSQL can store JSON documents; MongoDB and DynamoDB support transactions across multiple records; distributed SQL databases such as Spanner and CockroachDB can scale writes while keeping joins and transactions, with added latency and complexity."],"callout":"Choosing NoSQL before you need it can mean rebuilding joins and transactions in application code."},{"title":"Use the database that fits the data","body":["Real systems often combine database types. This is called <span class=\"term\">polyglot persistence</span>. For example, keep money and orders in a relational database for transactions, product details in a document store, and sessions in a key–value store. Start with one database and add another only when a kind of data has a clear need."],"callout":"Ask what the data needs: relationships, flexible fields, or a fast lookup by key."},{"title":"Search product descriptions","body":["A shopper types “red running shoes.” A SQL query such as <code>WHERE name LIKE '%red%'</code> can be slow over millions of rows. It also does not rank results, handle typos, or match “shoe” with “shoes.” A <span class=\"term\">search engine</span>, such as Elasticsearch or OpenSearch, builds an <span class=\"term\">inverted index</span>: for each word, it stores a list of documents that contain it. A stream of database changes updates this search index."],"callout":"The search index is a copy, not the source of truth. You can rebuild it from the main database."},{"title":"Add specialist stores only when needed","body":["A search engine is one example of a store built for a particular kind of work. Other specialized databases can be much better at one workload and worse at others. Add one when measurements show that a workload does not fit your current database. Each extra store takes work to run, secure, back up, and keep in sync."],"callout":"Many systems work well with one relational database for years. Add specialist databases one at a time for a measured need."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li><b>SQL databases</b> use tables and support joins and transactions; they are a safe default.</li><li><b>Document stores</b> keep flexible, self-contained records.</li><li><b>Key–value stores</b> look up a value by its key.</li><li>Choose a database to fit the data; one system may use several.</li><li>A <b>search index</b> is a copy fed from the source of truth.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Indexes</b> — why one query can take two seconds or two milliseconds."}]},"indexes":{"summary":"Learn how database indexes find rows quickly, and when an index cannot help.","goals":["Why a full table scan gets slower as a table grows","How a B-tree index finds a row","Why indexes add work to writes","How column order affects a composite index","When an index is not useful and how to check"],"steps":[{"title":"Finding one user takes two seconds","body":["Logging in requires finding a user by email. With 50 million users, the lookup takes over two seconds and gets slower as the table grows. Without an index, the database does a <span class=\"term\">full table scan</span>: it reads every table page and checks each row until it finds a match. Watch the amber request crawl along."],"callout":"A scan touches more data as the table grows. It may be fine for a thousand rows and slow for fifty million."},{"title":"Use a sorted guide","body":["You do not read a phone book page by page. Because it is sorted, you open near the middle and check whether the name comes before or after. That lets you ignore half the book. Repeat until you find the name. Halving 50 million entries takes about 26 steps."],"callout":"An index is a sorted guide to a column’s values, with a reference to each full row."},{"title":"Build a B-tree index","body":["The command <code>CREATE INDEX ON users (email)</code> builds a <span class=\"term\">B-tree</span>: a tree of sorted values. The top part points to the branch that covers an email; that branch points to the page with the row. This example takes about three page lookups instead of checking 50 million rows. The query now takes about 2 ms."],"callout":"Databases usually index primary keys automatically. Add indexes to columns you often search, join, or sort by."},{"title":"Why B-trees have few levels","body":["Each tree node fits on a disk page and holds hundreds of keys. Each level can point to hundreds of pages below it, so three or four levels can cover billions of rows. The top levels are used often and tend to stay in memory."],"callout":"That is why an indexed lookup can take about the same time with a million rows as with a billion."},{"title":"Indexes also slow down writes","body":["When a user signs up, the database writes the row to the table and updates every index for that table so its values stay sorted. Indexes also use disk and memory. Add indexes for queries you need; avoid adding them to every column."],"callout":"A table with six indexes must maintain the table and all six indexes for each insert. An unused index adds cost without helping a query."},{"title":"The order of columns matters","body":["An index can include several columns, such as <code>(country, city)</code>. It sorts by country first, then by city within each country—like a phone book sorted by last name and then first name. It can help a query on <code>country</code>, or on both <code>country</code> and <code>city</code>. A query on <code>city</code> alone cannot use this index and may scan the table."],"callout":"Put the column you filter by first. A multi-column index is most useful from its leftmost column onward."},{"title":"Some queries do not match the index","body":["Marketing asks for all Gmail users with <code>WHERE email LIKE '%@gmail.com'</code>. The index is sorted by the beginning of each email, but this query searches by the ending, so the database must scan the table. A function can cause the same problem: <code>WHERE LOWER(email) = …</code> may not use a plain index on <code>email</code>, because the index stores the original values."],"callout":"Possible fixes include an index on <code>LOWER(email)</code>, a separately stored and indexed email domain, or a search engine for free-text searches."},{"title":"An index can match too many rows","body":["Support asks for every user in the US: <code>WHERE country = 'US'</code>. An index on <code>country</code> may be ignored because 40% of rows match. Using the index would require visiting a different table page for each of 20 million rows, which can be slower than reading the table from start to finish. An index is <span class=\"term\">selective</span> when it narrows the search to a small share of the rows."],"callout":"If you often query a small group, such as <code>WHERE status = 'pending'</code>, a <b>partial index</b> can store only those rows. It is smaller and cheaper to maintain."},{"title":"Ask the database to explain","body":["Prefix a query with <code>EXPLAIN</code> to see the database’s plan: which index it expects to use and how many rows it expects to read. An “Index Scan” over a few rows is usually good. A “Seq Scan” over a large table may be a warning. <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> runs the query and reports actual timings."],"callout":"Most databases also keep a slow-query log. Start there to find queries that are actually taking time."},{"title":"Answer from the index alone","body":["The login page needs only a user’s name and ID. If the index also stores those fields, the database can return them without reading the table pages. This is a <span class=\"term\">covering index</span>. The diagram shows lookups ending at the index instead of reaching the table."],"callout":"In PostgreSQL, <code>CREATE INDEX ON users (email) INCLUDE (id, name)</code> adds those fields. It makes the index bigger, so use this for queries that matter."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Without an index, a lookup may <b>scan every row</b>.</li><li>A <b>B-tree</b> finds rows with a few page reads.</li><li>Every index adds work to <b>writes</b>; add indexes for queries you use.</li><li>Composite indexes work from the <b>leftmost column</b>.</li><li>Leading wildcards, functions, and low selectivity can prevent an index from helping. Check with <b>EXPLAIN</b>.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Storage Engines</b> — what the database does with a write, and why some engines suit reads while others suit writes."}]},"storage-engines":{"summary":"See how database storage engines save data safely, and why some handle reads or writes better.","goals":["Why a database uses a write-ahead log","How fsync, group commit, and checkpoints affect writes","How a B-tree engine reads and writes data","How an LSM-tree uses memtables, sorted files, filters, and compaction","How to choose between B-trees, LSM-trees, row stores, and column stores"],"steps":[{"title":"Fast memory and durable disk","body":["A database server can keep data in two places. <b>Memory</b> is very fast—an access may take nanoseconds—but its contents disappear if the process stops or power is lost. <b>Disk</b> keeps data through a crash, but making sure a write reached the device can take much longer. The database uses <code>fsync</code> to ask the operating system to confirm that data is on disk. A <span class=\"term\">storage engine</span> decides how the database uses memory and disk to make writes fast and safe."],"callout":"Data is stored in fixed-size <b>pages</b>, often 8 or 16 KB and holding many rows. The engine keeps frequently used pages in memory and reads other pages from disk when needed."},{"title":"Fast, but not safe: memory first","body":["A simple engine could change a page in memory, reply “saved,” and copy the page to disk every few seconds. Writes would be fast, but a power failure would erase changes made since the last copy—including orders users were told had succeeded. A failure during a write could also leave a partly written page that cannot be read. This is a <span class=\"term\">torn page</span>."],"callout":"When a database says “saved,” it should mean the change survives a crash. This is <b>durability</b>, the D in ACID."},{"title":"Record the change before replying","body":["Writing a whole 8 KB page before every reply would be safe but slow, even when the change is only one 100-byte row. Instead, first append a short description of the change to a log file and use <code>fsync</code> to confirm it reached disk. This is the <span class=\"term\">write-ahead log</span> (WAL). Appending to the end of a file is efficient. The database can update its in-memory pages now and copy them to their normal disk locations later. <small>The speeds shown are for one ordinary SSD. Your device may differ; the relative trade-offs are what matter.</small>"],"callout":"After a crash, the engine replays the log to restore changes it had confirmed. The log is the record of truth; the pages are a convenient copy of that data."},{"title":"How often must the disk confirm?","body":["On an SSD, waiting for the log to reach disk takes a fraction of a millisecond. By default, a database may do this for each transaction. At thousands of commits per second, that wait can become the limit. <span class=\"term\">Group commit</span> lets several transactions that arrive together share one <code>fsync</code>. Some databases can also sync on a timer, such as every 100 ms or every second. This can increase throughput, but a crash may lose the newest writes that were not yet synced."],"callout":"Know the setting your database uses. Redis in append-only mode syncs once a second by default. PostgreSQL with <code>synchronous_commit = off</code> and MySQL with <code>innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit = 2</code> can also lose the newest writes after a power cut."},{"title":"Keep the log from growing forever","body":["The log keeps every change, so it would grow without limit if the database never cleaned it up. Recovery would also take longer because the engine would have to replay the whole log. Every few minutes, the engine takes a <span class=\"term\">checkpoint</span>: it copies changed pages into the data files, records how far it got, and can then remove older log entries. After a crash, the database only needs to replay changes since the last checkpoint."],"callout":"A checkpoint writes many changed pages to different disk locations. Databases spread this work out; if it falls behind, users may see periodic slowdowns."},{"title":"B-trees: update the page in place","body":["PostgreSQL, MySQL with InnoDB, SQL Server, and Oracle use this classic design. Data sits in fixed-size pages, located with <b>B-trees</b> (see <em>Indexes</em>); in some databases the table itself is a B-tree. To change a row, the engine finds its page and updates that page <em>in place</em>. The write-ahead log protects the change. Reads are predictable: finding a row takes about three or four page reads, often from memory. Nearby keys are stored on nearby pages, so reading a range is efficient."],"callout":"Each row has a known place. That makes reads predictable, but every write must find and update that place."},{"title":"When many small writes arrive","body":["Now imagine sensors, chat messages, or clicks arriving as 200,000 small writes per second, each for a different key. Each change affects a different page. The database keeps those changed pages in memory, then eventually writes each whole page back to its place on disk—even if only a few bytes changed. The log can keep up because it is written in order. The scattered page writes cannot, so checkpoints fall behind."],"callout":"This is <b>write amplification</b>: saving 100 bytes can require writing a log record and an 8 KB page. A write-heavy workload can make the disk write many times more data than the app sent."},{"title":"LSM-trees: write sorted files in batches","body":["A different design avoids finding and changing a row’s disk page. A write goes into the log and a sorted structure in memory called a <span class=\"term\">memtable</span>. When the memtable fills, the database writes it to disk in one ordered pass as an immutable sorted file called an <span class=\"term\">SSTable</span>, then starts a new memtable. This is a <span class=\"term\">log-structured merge tree</span> (LSM-tree), used by Cassandra, HBase, ScyllaDB, and RocksDB."],"callout":"An update creates a newer value for the same key. A delete creates a marker called a <b>tombstone</b>. Existing files are not changed in place."},{"title":"Finding a key across several files","body":["Reading can now take more work. The latest value for a key might be in memory or in any SSTable. The engine checks files from newest to oldest. To avoid reading every file, each SSTable has a <b>Bloom filter</b> that can say the key is definitely not there, plus a small index that points to the right key range. A typical lookup can then read from one file."],"callout":"B-trees do more work during writes so reads stay predictable. LSM-trees keep writes simple, but may need more work to read. Every storage engine makes a trade-off between the two."},{"title":"Merge files in the background","body":["SSTables pile up, and older files may contain values that have since changed or been deleted. A background process called <span class=\"term\">compaction</span> merges sorted files: it reads a few files, writes a new file with only the latest value for each key, then removes the old files. This limits how many files a read must check and frees space. The cost is that data is rewritten several times and compaction shares disk time with live requests."],"callout":"If writes arrive faster than compaction can process them, files pile up, reads slow down, and the disk can fill. Watch the compaction backlog."},{"title":"B-tree or LSM-tree?","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>B-tree</th><th>LSM-tree</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Writes</td><td>Scattered page updates</td><td>Sequential appends</td></tr><tr><td>Reads</td><td>A few pages, predictable</td><td>Several files; filters help</td></tr><tr><td>Space</td><td>Pages left part-empty</td><td>Compact, compresses well</td></tr><tr><td>Background</td><td>Checkpoints</td><td>Compaction</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>PostgreSQL, MySQL</td><td>Cassandra, RocksDB</td></tr></tbody></table>For frequent reads, range queries, and transactions, a B-tree may fit well. For a huge workload with many writes that mostly add new records, an LSM-tree may fit better. Some SQL databases, including CockroachDB, TiDB, and MyRocks, use an LSM-tree underneath."],"callout":"You usually choose a database, not its storage engine directly. Knowing the engine helps explain how the database behaves under load."},{"title":"Store rows or columns?","body":["Both designs so far store each <b>row</b> together. That works well when the app reads or changes whole records. But an analyst may ask <code>SELECT SUM(price) FROM orders</code> across two billion rows. A row store may have to read all 40 columns in every row to add up one column. A <span class=\"term\">column store</span> keeps each column separately, so this query reads only <code>price</code>—about one-fortieth of the data. Similar values also compress well. This is how warehouses such as BigQuery, Snowflake, Redshift, and ClickHouse scan billions of rows quickly."],"callout":"Row stores suit apps with many small reads and writes. Column stores suit analytics with a few very large scans. Systems often copy data from one to the other; see <em>Batch &amp; Stream Processing</em>."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Memory is fast but temporary; disk is slower and keeps data through a crash. The storage engine works between them.</li><li>The <b>write-ahead log</b> records a change on disk before the database confirms it.</li><li>How often the database waits for <b>fsync</b> affects both speed and what may be lost in a crash.</li><li><b>B-trees</b> update pages in place: reads are predictable, writes can be scattered.</li><li><b>LSM-trees</b> append writes: writes are fast, while reads and compaction need extra work.</li><li><b>Column stores</b> read only the columns an analytics query needs.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Replication</b> — what to do when one database server cannot handle all the reads or might fail."}]},"replication":{"summary":"Copy a database to other machines to serve more reads and keep working through failures.","goals":["How a leader sends changes to followers","How replicas can serve more reads","What replication lag means for users","How to fail over when the leader fails","When to use analytics replicas, backups, and other replication styles"],"steps":[{"title":"One database handles every request","body":["Every read and write goes to one database server. It is 90% busy, mostly with reads such as product pages, profiles, and feeds. It is also a <span class=\"term\">single point of failure</span>: if it stops, the whole app loses its database."],"callout":"Many apps do far more reads than writes—often 10 or 100 reads for every write."},{"title":"Keep database copies","body":["Add two <span class=\"term\">followers</span>, also called replicas. The <span class=\"term\">leader</span> continues to handle every write and sends each change to the followers in a <span class=\"term\">replication log</span>. Each follower applies the changes and copies the leader’s data."],"callout":"Leader and follower are also called primary and replica. Most major databases support this setup."},{"title":"Send reads to followers","body":["The app now sends reads to the followers and writes to the leader. The two followers share the read work, and the leader’s CPU use falls from 90% to 30%. If read traffic grows, you can add another follower."],"callout":"This helps apps with many reads. It does not increase write capacity because writes still go to one leader."},{"title":"A follower can be behind","body":["Replication is often <span class=\"term\">asynchronous</span>: the leader confirms a write before sending it to the followers. A short time later, each follower applies the change. Alice changes her name and reloads the page. If that read goes to a follower that has not caught up, she sees her old name and may think the save failed."],"callout":"Replication lag is often milliseconds, but under load it can grow to seconds or minutes."},{"title":"Let users read their own changes","body":["One fix is to send a user’s reads to the leader for a few seconds after they write. Other users can keep reading from followers. Another option is <span class=\"term\">synchronous replication</span>: the leader waits for a follower to confirm the change before saying “saved.” This avoids lag on that follower, but makes writes slower and can stop writes if the follower is down."],"callout":"For important data, some systems use one synchronous follower for safety and asynchronous followers for speed. Fully asynchronous systems may lose the newest writes if the leader fails before copying them."},{"title":"The leader stops","body":["The leader crashes. The followers can still serve reads, but writes fail until a new leader is chosen. Users cannot sign up, post, or pay."],"callout":"Followers can keep reads available. To resume writes, the system needs a new leader."},{"title":"Fail over to a follower","body":["<span class=\"term\">Failover</span> means switching work to a backup after a failure. Promote the most up-to-date follower to leader, point writes to it, and have the other follower copy from it. Add a new follower to restore the spare. Writes can resume in seconds to a minute. Changes that had not reached a follower may be lost. The difficult part is knowing whether the old leader is truly dead. If it is only slow or disconnected, both machines might accept writes; this is <span class=\"term\">split brain</span>. A <span class=\"term\">majority vote</span> helps the group agree on one leader. See <em>Consensus &amp; Leader Election</em>."],"callout":"This is like a standby load balancer taking over. Managed databases can automate failover."},{"title":"Run heavy reports on a separate copy","body":["The finance team runs a monthly report that scans years of orders and takes 20 minutes. When it runs on a follower that also serves app reads, the follower slows down and falls minutes behind. Use a dedicated <b>analytics replica</b> that the app does not read from, so large reports do not slow normal requests."],"callout":"At larger scale, copy data into an analytics warehouse such as BigQuery, Snowflake, or Redshift, built for large scans."},{"title":"Replicas copy mistakes too","body":["A software change runs <code>UPDATE users SET email = NULL</code> without a <code>WHERE</code> condition. The leader updates every user, then replication quickly copies the same mistake to every follower. Replicas help when a machine fails, but they do not undo a bad write. For that, keep <span class=\"term\">backups</span>: a snapshot plus archived write logs can rebuild the database as it looked at an earlier time. This is <span class=\"term\">point-in-time recovery</span>."],"callout":"A backup is useful only if it can be restored. Practice restoring and measure how long it takes."},{"title":"Three ways to copy changes","body":["So far, the system has been <b>single-leader</b>: one machine handles writes and others handle reads. It is simple and common in SQL databases. <b>Multi-leader</b> replication has a leader in each region, so writes can stay nearby, but simultaneous edits can conflict. <b>Leaderless</b> systems write to and read from several copies. They use <span class=\"term\">quorums</span>, a rule for how many copies must respond; the CAP lesson covers this."],"callout":"Use single-leader replication until you have a concrete reason—such as writes in multiple regions or very high availability—to handle conflicts."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>A <b>leader</b> handles writes and sends changes to <b>followers</b>.</li><li>Followers can serve more reads and keep data available if a machine fails.</li><li>Asynchronous followers may <b>lag</b>; send a user’s reads to the leader right after a write.</li><li>When the leader fails, <b>fail over</b> to a follower.</li><li>Keep heavy analytics on a separate replica.</li><li>Replicas are <b>not backups</b>; keep backups and practice restoring them.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Sharding</b> — when one leader cannot keep up with the writes."}]},"sharding":{"summary":"Split data across machines when one database can no longer hold it or handle its writes.","goals":["Why replicas do not increase write capacity","How range and hash shard keys divide data","How hot keys overload one shard","Why queries across shards cost more","Why adding a shard can move data"],"steps":[{"title":"One machine is too small","body":["The users database is 20 TB and handles 40,000 writes each second. Followers can help with reads, but every write still goes through one leader and all data must fit on its disks. The largest machine is already in use. Copying all that data for a backup, restore, or new replica now takes most of a day."],"callout":"Replication copies data. To spread writes and storage, split the data itself."},{"title":"Split the database into shards","body":["<span class=\"term\">Sharding</span> divides one large database into smaller databases called <b>shards</b>. Each shard stores part of the data and handles writes for that part. A <span class=\"term\">shard key</span> decides where each row goes. Here, rows are grouped by user ID range. The app or a router checks the range and sends a query to the right shard."],"callout":"Shards are usually replicated too: shards spread writes; followers within each shard spread reads and help survive failures."},{"title":"Ranges can create hot spots","body":["User IDs increase over time, so new signups keep landing in the last range. If new users are the most active, shard D gets most of the writes while A is mostly idle. Dates and alphabetical names can create the same problem: newly arriving data piles onto one shard. Some range-partitioned databases, such as Bigtable, HBase, and CockroachDB, split busy ranges automatically. But if writes always go to the newest end, that last range can still be busiest."],"callout":"Choose a shard key that spreads both the stored data and the requests."},{"title":"Hash the shard key","body":["Instead of putting nearby IDs together, pass each ID through a <span class=\"term\">hash function</span>, which turns an input into a number that appears spread out. Then choose a shard with <code>hash(user_id) % 4</code>. Consecutive IDs now go to different shards, which helps spread signups and their traffic."],"callout":"Hashing spreads IDs but makes range queries harder. “Find IDs 1–1000” may need to ask every shard. This is often a useful trade-off."},{"title":"Case: one celebrity overloads a shard","body":["Hashing spreads keys evenly, but it cannot make one key less popular. A pop star with 50 million followers is stored on shard C, just like any other user. But every profile view and like for that star goes to C. C is overloaded while A, B, and D are fine. This is a <span class=\"term\">hot key</span>, also called the celebrity problem."],"callout":"A shard key cannot spread requests for one single key across machines by itself."},{"title":"Cache hot reads or split hot writes","body":["For a key with many <b>reads</b>, put a cache or several cache copies in front of its shard. Then most profile views do not reach the shard. For a key with many <b>writes</b>, such as a celebrity’s like counter, split it into smaller keys: write to <code>likes:42#0</code> through <code>likes:42#7</code> at random across shards, then add them up when reading."],"callout":"Social networks and ticketing systems often detect their most popular keys and handle those keys separately."},{"title":"A query may need every shard","body":["Finding one user is fast: hash the ID and ask one shard. But “show the top 10 users by follower count” is not based on the user ID, so the app asks <b>every</b> shard and combines the answers. This is called <span class=\"term\">scatter–gather</span>. The query waits for the slowest shard, and joining data stored on different shards is difficult."],"callout":"Choose a shard key that matches important queries, so a query can often go to just one shard."},{"title":"Adding a shard moves many rows","body":["The shards fill up, so you add a fifth. But changing <code>hash % 4</code> to <code>hash % 5</code> changes the destination for about 80% of users. Terabytes of data must move between shards while the system continues serving traffic."],"callout":"With <code>hash % N</code>, changing N reshuffles most keys. Consistent hashing, the next lesson, reduces how many move."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li><b>Sharding</b> splits stored data and writes across machines.</li><li>The <b>shard key</b> chooses where each row lives; it should spread data and requests.</li><li><b>Ranges</b> can create hot spots. <b>Hashes</b> spread keys but make range lookups touch more shards.</li><li>Cache hot reads or split writes for a <b>hot key</b>.</li><li>Queries that do not match the shard key may ask <b>every shard</b>. Changing <code>% N</code> moves most rows.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Consistent Hashing</b> — add or remove a shard while moving only some of the keys."}]},"consistent-hashing":{"summary":"Add or remove servers while moving only the keys that need a new home.","goals":["Why hash modulo N moves most keys when N changes","How servers and keys are placed on a hash ring","How adding or removing a server moves one part of the ring","How virtual nodes balance the load","How to store copies around the ring and use it for caches"],"steps":[{"title":"Changing N remaps most keys","body":["With <code>shard = hash(key) % N</code>, changing four shards to five changes the destination for about 80% of keys. Nearly all the data may need to move. We want a method where a new server takes only the keys in its share of the data—about 1/N when the load is balanced."],"callout":"The idea is to choose a key’s server without using the number of servers."},{"title":"Make the hash values a ring","body":["A <span class=\"term\">hash function</span> turns a name or key into a number. Imagine all possible hash values arranged in a circle, with the largest value connected back to zero. This is a <span class=\"term\">hash ring</span>. Hash each server’s name too, then place it at that point on the ring. The hash decides its location, so servers may not be evenly spaced."],"callout":"The server’s location does not depend on how many servers are in the system."},{"title":"Give each key the next server","body":["Hash each data key onto the same ring. Starting from the key’s position, move clockwise to the first server. That server owns the key. The colored arcs show each server’s share; each dot is a key. To find a server, clients can search a sorted list of server positions with binary search."],"callout":"Hash the key, move clockwise, and stop at the first server."},{"title":"One new server takes one arc","body":["Server E joins the ring between C and B. It takes the keys in the arc just before its position—the keys that used to pass E and go to C. All other keys keep their current server. The pulsing keys are the ones that move."],"callout":"With an even share, adding a fifth server moves about one-fifth of the keys."},{"title":"Removing a server moves one arc","body":["Return to four servers, then remove C. Keys that belonged to C continue clockwise to D. The other keys stay where they are. With <code>% N</code>, removing a server would change the destination for almost every key."],"callout":"Dynamo, Cassandra, caches, and CDNs use this approach or a close relative. Rendezvous and jump hashing can get a similar result without using a ring."},{"title":"Random positions can be uneven","body":["A server’s hash determines its position, so the ring arcs can have different sizes. After C leaves, D owns a much larger arc and receives more keys and traffic than A or B. With only a few servers, a random placement can leave one server doing twice as much work as another."],"callout":"Uneven ring shares mean uneven load, which can create the hot spots sharding was meant to avoid."},{"title":"Use virtual nodes to balance shares","body":["Give each physical server several positions on the ring. These positions are <span class=\"term\">virtual nodes</span>, made by hashing names such as <code>A#1</code> and <code>A#2</code>. Each server now owns many small arcs instead of one large arc. This makes the shares more even. When a server leaves, its small arcs are spread across many servers rather than all landing on one neighbor."],"callout":"Real systems may use 16 to a few hundred virtual nodes for each server. A stronger server can receive more positions."},{"title":"Keep copies on nearby servers","body":["One copy would be lost if its server failed. Instead, store each key on the first three distinct servers clockwise from it: the owner and the next two. In the diagram, the key at 160° has copies on A, B, and C. If A fails, B and C still have the data, and the next server takes over A’s share."],"callout":"Dynamo calls these servers a preference list. With virtual nodes, skip later positions that belong to a server already chosen."},{"title":"Grow a cache cluster","body":["A group of Memcached servers sits in front of a database. With <code>hash(key) % N</code>, adding a fifth server remaps about 80% of keys. Those keys miss the cache at once, sending a rush of requests to the database. With consistent hashing, only about 20% of keys move, so most cache entries stay warm and the database sees less extra work."],"callout":"Consistent hashing first became popular for spreading web caches across servers that can be added or removed."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Place servers and keys on a <b>hash ring</b>; each key belongs to the next server clockwise.</li><li>Adding or removing a server changes the owner of one arc of keys.</li><li><b>Virtual nodes</b> make server shares more even and spread a failed server’s keys across the rest.</li><li>Keep copies on the next few distinct servers for redundancy.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Unique IDs at Scale</b> — how to name every row when there is no single database to count for you."}]},"unique-ids":{"summary":"Give every row a unique name across many servers without making all inserts wait for one counter.","goals":["Why a database counter stops working after sharding","How ticket services and ID blocks work","Why random UUIDs affect database indexes","How UUID v7 and Snowflake create time-sortable IDs","What clock changes and public IDs can reveal"],"steps":[{"title":"Give each row a unique ID","body":["Orders, users, and payments each need an <b>identifier</b> that is unique across the system. Other rows may refer to it; it can appear in a URL or be read to support staff. With one database, an <code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code> or <code>SERIAL</code> column can hand out 1001, 1002, 1003, and so on, without repeating an ID."],"callout":"A counter also gives small IDs that sort by creation time. Each alternative keeps some of these benefits and gives up others."},{"title":"Two shards, two counters","body":["The orders table is now split across database shards. Each shard has its own counter, and both have just issued ID <b>1004</b>. Two orders now share an ID. A link to <code>/orders/1004</code>, a cache entry, or a refund could refer to either one. The same collision would cause problems if shards were later merged or split again."],"callout":"IDs must be unique across the whole system, not just within one server."},{"title":"Use one service to issue IDs","body":["Move the counter to a small <span class=\"term\">ticket server</span> whose job is to hand out the next number. Before inserting a row, an app server asks it for an ID. IDs stay unique and roughly sorted by time, but every insert adds a network round trip. If the ticket server is unavailable, new rows cannot be created."],"callout":"Flickr used two ticket servers: one issued odd IDs and one even IDs. Either could fail while the other kept issuing IDs."},{"title":"Reserve blocks of IDs","body":["Instead of asking for one number at a time, each app server reserves a <b>block</b> of 1,000 IDs in one request and then uses them from memory. That is one round trip per thousand inserts, and a server can continue for a while if the ticket server is down. IDs from different servers may not follow exact creation order. A crashed server can also leave unused IDs in its block, creating gaps."],"callout":"Gaps are usually harmless. If invoice numbers must have no gaps by law, use a separate deliberate process; do not rely on ordinary IDs for that."},{"title":"Generate a random UUID","body":["A <span class=\"term\">UUID</span> (universally unique identifier) version 4 uses 122 random bits. Any server, phone, or script can create one without asking another service. Two IDs could match in theory, but a collision is extraordinarily unlikely: at a billion UUIDs each second, it would take about 86 years to reach a 50% chance of any repeat."],"callout":"A UUID is 128 bits—twice the size of a typical 64-bit ID. IDs are copied into indexes and other rows, so that larger size uses more storage."},{"title":"Random IDs scatter index writes","body":["A primary-key index is often a B-tree sorted by ID (see <em>Indexes</em>). Counter IDs go to the last page, which can stay in memory. Random IDs can land on any page. Once the index is too large for memory, inserts may need to read cold pages from disk and split pages in many parts of the tree. Inserts can become several times slower, and pages may be left half empty."],"callout":"This effect is strongest when the table itself is stored in primary-key order, as in MySQL InnoDB and SQL Server, but random IDs can affect any B-tree."},{"title":"Put a timestamp at the front","body":["Keep the ID unique, but put a timestamp before its random bits. <span class=\"term\">UUID v7</span> stores a 48-bit millisecond timestamp first; <span class=\"term\">ULID</span> is a similar time-sortable ID. New IDs sort roughly by creation time, so inserts tend to go to the right-hand end of the index. They do not need a central counter."],"callout":"For UUID primary keys, UUID v7 is often a better fit for indexes than UUID v4. Use a generator with monotonic behavior if IDs created in the same millisecond must also sort in creation order. UUID v4 is useful when random, hard-to-guess IDs are needed."},{"title":"Pack time and machine into a Snowflake ID","body":["Twitter needed IDs that fit in 64 bits, sort by time, and could be made many times per second without a central counter. Its <span class=\"term\">Snowflake</span> design puts three fields in one number: a timestamp in milliseconds, the ID generator’s machine ID, and a sequence number for IDs created during that millisecond. Different machines have different machine IDs; one machine increments its sequence so it does not reuse a number."],"callout":"A machine can make 4,096 IDs per millisecond in the example format. Discord, Instagram, and others use similar designs with different bit allocations."},{"title":"Protect against a clock moving backward","body":["Snowflake IDs use the machine’s clock. If time sync corrects a clock that was two seconds fast by moving it backward, the machine could reuse timestamps and issue duplicate IDs. A generator can remember the last timestamp it used and wait—or refuse to issue IDs—until the clock catches up."],"callout":"Two machines must not share the same machine ID either. Assign IDs from one place, such as a configuration service, or derive them from another unique value."},{"title":"IDs can reveal information","body":["IDs often appear in URLs, where people can read them. If a competitor sees order #1004 on Monday and #1250 on Tuesday, they can estimate that you handled about 246 orders in between. Sequential IDs are also easy to guess: changing <code>/invoices/1004</code> to <code>/invoices/1005</code> may reveal another record. A timestamp in an ID can reveal when it was created."],"callout":"Use a separate random public ID if you need to hide order volume or timing. Still check authorization on every request (see <em>Authorization</em>): an ID that is hard to guess does not grant access."},{"title":"Choose an ID for the job","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Size</th><th>Time-sorted</th><th>Needs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>DB counter</b></td><td>64 bits</td><td>Yes</td><td>One database</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ticket blocks</b></td><td>64 bits</td><td>Roughly</td><td>A ticket service</td></tr><tr><td><b>UUID v4</b></td><td>128 bits</td><td>No</td><td>Nothing</td></tr><tr><td><b>UUID v7</b></td><td>128 bits</td><td>Roughly</td><td>A clock</td></tr><tr><td><b>Snowflake</b></td><td>64 bits</td><td>Yes</td><td>Machine IDs + clock</td></tr></tbody></table>For one database, use its counter. For many independent writers, UUID v7 avoids a central service. For compact numeric IDs at high volume, Snowflake is an option."],"callout":"There is no best ID for every system. Choose the size, ordering, coordination, and privacy properties you need."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>A database counter stops being unique when several shards issue IDs independently.</li><li>A <b>ticket server</b> restores one shared counter; blocks reduce the number of requests to that service.</li><li><b>UUID v4</b> needs no coordination but scatters index writes. <b>UUID v7</b> puts a timestamp first, so it sorts roughly by time.</li><li><b>Snowflake</b> combines a timestamp, machine ID, and sequence in a 64-bit ID. Protect it from clock rollback.</li><li>IDs can reveal volume and timing. They are never a substitute for authorization checks.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>CAP &amp; Consistency</b> — what copies of data should do when the network between them breaks."}]},"cap":{"summary":"When replicas cannot communicate, decide whether to reject requests or answer with possibly stale data.","goals":["What it means when a network partition separates replicas","How consistency and availability trade off during a partition","How eventual consistency and conflicts work","How read and write quorums overlap","How to choose consistency for each operation"],"steps":[{"title":"Keep a copy in each region","body":["A ticket site stores data in Europe and the US so users in either region can get a fast answer. Each region copies changes to the other. Both copies currently agree that one seat is left for a concert."],"callout":"Copies in multiple regions can make responses faster and help the service survive a regional failure. They also need a way to stay in sync."},{"title":"The regions lose contact","body":["An undersea cable is cut. Both regions and their users still work, but the regions cannot communicate with one another. This is a <span class=\"term\">network partition</span>. A US user now wants the last ticket, but the US copy cannot check the Europe copy. What should it do?"],"callout":"Partitions can happen because of damaged cables, failed routers, configuration mistakes, or overloaded network links."},{"title":"Option 1: reject uncertain requests","body":["The site can refuse requests that it cannot confirm. Europe has the primary copy and continues to serve users. The US side rejects writes—and reads that might show old data—until the link is restored. No user gets an answer based on conflicting data, but US users see errors during the partition."],"callout":"Favor consistency when an incorrect answer could cost money, such as for payments, balances, inventory, or ticket sales."},{"title":"Option 2: answer from each copy","body":["The site can let both regions keep accepting requests using their local copy. Ana in Paris buys the last ticket. Ben in Boston also buys the last ticket. Both users get an answer, but the copies now disagree and the site has sold one seat twice."],"callout":"Favor availability when a brief disagreement is acceptable, such as for likes, view counts, carts, or social feeds."},{"title":"Bring the copies back together","body":["The cable is repaired and the regions exchange the changes they missed. If left alone, the copies will eventually match; this is <span class=\"term\">eventual consistency</span>. But the system must still resolve conflicting writes. <b>Last write wins</b> is simple, but it could silently discard one ticket sale. The application could instead keep both sales, refund one, and explain what happened."],"callout":"Many databases use last write wins by default. It can lose one update quietly, so use it only when that is acceptable."},{"title":"Which write was last?","body":["Last write wins needs to compare write times, but each server has its own clock and clocks can drift. Here the US clock is three seconds fast. A customer saves a delivery address in the US, then corrects it two seconds later in Europe. The correction is newer, but its timestamp looks earlier, so last write wins may keep the old address."],"callout":"Do not rely only on wall-clock time to order events across machines. Systems can use <b>logical clocks</b> that count event order, keep version information, or let the application merge both changes."},{"title":"What the CAP theorem says","body":["The <span class=\"term\">CAP theorem</span> describes a choice during a network <b>partition</b> (P). A replicated system must give up either <b>consistency</b> (every read returns the latest write, as if there were one copy) or <b>availability</b> (every request to a working server gets a response). This is not simply “pick two of three”: partitions can happen, so the real choice is what the system does while copies cannot communicate. <small>CAP consistency is often called <b>linearizability</b>. It is different from the C in ACID, which means a transaction preserves the data’s rules.</small>"],"callout":"PACELC adds a trade-off that also exists when there is no partition: coordinating copies can add latency. Consistency has a cost."},{"title":"Tune the trade-off with quorums","body":["Some databases let each request choose how many copies must respond. Let <b>N</b> be the number of copies, <b>W</b> the number that must confirm a write, and <b>R</b> the number a read asks. With N = 3, W = 2, and R = 2, the read and write groups overlap because W + R > N. The read can find a copy that has the latest write and return it instead of a stale value."],"callout":"W = 1 and R = 1 are faster but can return stale data. W = 3 requires all copies and can fail if one is unreachable. Cassandra, Riak, and ScyllaDB expose these choices; DynamoDB offers simpler consistency options."},{"title":"Different writes need different promises","body":["A like can be confirmed by one copy and spread to others later (W = 1, R = 1). It stays fast and available, and a briefly wrong count is usually acceptable. A payment in this example waits for a majority on both write and read (W = 2, R = 2). That can be slower and may fail when two copies cannot be reached, but it avoids reading an old “unpaid” value."],"callout":"Choose consistency per operation. Ask what it would cost if the answer were briefly wrong."},{"title":"Consistency has several levels","body":["Consistency is not only “strong” or “eventual.” <b>Linearizable</b> reads always see the latest write. Other systems may promise <b>read-your-writes</b> (you see your own changes), <b>monotonic reads</b> (you do not see older data later), or <b>causal consistency</b> (a reply does not appear before the message it answers). Weaker promises can be cheaper and more available. Choose the weakest promise your users can accept."],"callout":"Many eventually consistent systems offer read-your-writes within one session, which is enough for many apps."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>A <b>partition</b> separates copies that may still serve users.</li><li>During a partition, choose whether to reject uncertain requests for <b>consistency</b> or answer for <b>availability</b>.</li><li>When copies reconnect, they may need to resolve conflicts. Do not trust unsynchronized wall clocks to order writes.</li><li><b>Quorums</b> use W + R > N to make read and write groups overlap.</li><li>Choose the weakest consistency promise each operation can safely use.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Consensus &amp; Leader Election</b> — how servers agree on one truth when some cannot be reached."}]},"consensus":{"summary":"See how a group of servers agrees on one leader and one history, even when some cannot communicate.","goals":["Why unsafe failover can create two leaders","How majority votes overlap","How Raft elections use terms and votes","When a write is committed","How cluster size affects failures","How fencing tokens stop an old leader"],"steps":[{"title":"One leader and two followers","body":["From <em>Replication</em>: one <span class=\"term\">leader</span> handles writes and copies them to its followers. If the leader fails, a follower can take over. But how can the others tell that the leader failed? What if it is only slow or disconnected?"],"callout":"A crashed server, a slow server, and a server behind a broken network link can all look the same to other machines: no response."},{"title":"Two leaders create two histories","body":["A network problem cuts server A off from B and C. A is still running and serving App Server 1. B gets no message from A, assumes it failed, and takes over. Now both accept writes. App Server 1 sells seat 7 to Ana through A; another sells it to Ben through B. These two conflicting histories are a <span class=\"term\">split brain</span>."],"callout":"A timeout shows that a server did not respond in time. It cannot prove the server has stopped."},{"title":"Require a majority for decisions","body":["A safe rule is that electing a leader and confirming a write both need agreement from <b>more than half</b> of the nodes. With three nodes, two votes are needed. A, alone, cannot confirm a write. B and C together can elect a leader and continue. Any two majorities share at least one node, so two groups cannot both form separate majorities at the same time."],"callout":"During a partition, the smaller side stops accepting writes. This keeps the data consistent, but reduces availability on that side."},{"title":"Elect a leader with Raft","body":["<span class=\"term\">Raft</span> is an algorithm for choosing a leader and keeping a shared log. The leader sends regular <b>heartbeats</b>. Each follower starts a random timer; if it gets no heartbeat before the timer ends, it becomes a <b>candidate</b>. B’s timer ends first. It starts <b>term 2</b>, votes for itself, and asks the others to vote. Each node votes once per term, and only for a candidate whose log is at least as up to date as its own. C votes for B, giving B two of three votes. B becomes leader for term 2."],"callout":"Random timers make simultaneous elections less likely. If votes split, no candidate wins; timers restart and another term begins. This usually takes about a second."},{"title":"Confirm a write with a majority","body":["Writes use the same majority rule. App Server 2 sends <code>seat 7 → Ben</code> to B. B adds the change to its <span class=\"term\">log</span> and sends it to the followers. Once B and C have stored it, a majority has the entry and the write is <b>committed</b>. Only then does B apply it and reply “saved.” The committed entry survives a failure as long as a majority remains."],"callout":"Each node applies the same log entries in the same order, so the nodes reach the same state. This is a <b>replicated state machine</b>."},{"title":"The old leader steps down","body":["The network link returns. A, which still thinks it is leader for term 1, receives a heartbeat from B marked term 2. The higher term wins, so A becomes a follower. A has an entry for Ana’s booking that never reached a majority, so it was never confirmed. A removes that entry and copies B’s log. Now all three nodes have the same history."],"callout":"Terms are logical counters used to order elections. Servers compare term numbers, not wall-clock times."},{"title":"Choose the number of nodes","body":["A cluster of <b>N</b> nodes needs ⌊N/2⌋ + 1 votes for a majority. It can keep working when up to ⌊(N − 1)/2⌋ nodes are down. Three nodes can survive one failure; five can survive two. Four nodes still need three votes, so they can survive only one failure—just like three nodes. Put nodes in separate availability zones so one outage does not take out a majority."],"callout":"Three or five nodes are common. Each write waits for more acknowledgements as the cluster grows, so a larger cluster can be slower without surviving more failures."},{"title":"A leader pauses, then wakes up","body":["Leader B freezes for 20 seconds, perhaps during a long garbage-collection pause or while its virtual machine moves. A and C stop receiving heartbeats, elect C for term 3, and continue. B wakes up and still believes it is leader. Before it receives a message that corrects it, B writes to shared storage over C’s newer work."],"callout":"A paused machine does not know it was paused. Checking “am I leader?” earlier does not prove it is still leader now."},{"title":"Fence out the old leader","body":["Every leader request can carry its <b>term number</b> as a <span class=\"term\">fencing token</span>. The storage remembers the highest token it has seen. C has already written with token 3. When B’s write arrives with token 2, storage rejects it. The resource itself prevents the old leader from changing data."],"callout":"Distributed locks need the same protection: give each new lock holder a larger number, and make the protected resource check it. A lock that only says “you own this” is not enough."},{"title":"The cost of consensus","body":["Every write waits for a round trip to a majority. Inside one data center this may take one or two milliseconds; across regions it can add 50–150 ms to every write. All writes also pass through one leader, which limits throughput to what that machine can handle. If the cluster cannot reach a majority, it accepts no writes. That is deliberate."],"callout":"Consensus trades latency and availability for correctness. Use it for critical decisions, not every like or page view."},{"title":"Use consensus tools instead of building one","body":["Most teams do not implement Raft or Paxos themselves. They run a small consensus system such as <b>etcd</b>, <b>ZooKeeper</b>, or <b>Consul</b> to store critical facts: who the leader is, what the configuration says, or who holds a lock. Other systems depend on it. Kubernetes stores its cluster state in etcd. Kafka elects controllers with Raft. CockroachDB, Spanner, and MongoDB use consensus inside database groups to decide who leads."],"callout":"Consensus is the machinery that makes “promote the most up-to-date follower” safe during replication failover."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>A timeout cannot tell a <b>failed</b> server from an <b>unreachable</b> one. Unsafe failover can create split brain.</li><li><b>Majority votes</b> are needed for elections and writes; two majorities overlap.</li><li><b>Terms</b> order leaders. A higher term makes an old leader step down.</li><li>A write is <b>committed</b> after a majority has stored it.</li><li>Use <b>three or five nodes</b> across zones, and use <b>fencing tokens</b> for writes outside the shared log.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Transactions &amp; Isolation</b> — keeping one database correct when many requests change data at once."}]},"transactions":{"summary":"Learn how databases protect shared records when several requests read and change them at once.","goals":["How read-then-write races can sell the same item twice","How row locks and atomic updates prevent races","How version checks and isolation levels work","What phantom rows and write skew mean","How deadlocks happen and how constraints help","How short transactions can use expiring holds"],"steps":[{"title":"There is one seat left","body":["Ana and Ben both want the last concert seat. Their requests reach different app servers, which share one database. The booking code reads how many seats are left, then writes a booking if the answer is one."],"callout":"Each request works correctly on its own. The bug appears only when both requests run at the same time."},{"title":"Both requests book it","body":["Both servers read “1 seat left” before either writes. Both decide the seat is free, both create a booking, and both send a confirmation. One seat has two tickets. This is a <span class=\"term\">race condition</span>: the outcome depends on which request runs first. In this example, one write overwrites the other’s update to the count."],"callout":"Races can be hard to reproduce in tests and appear in production when traffic is high, such as during a ticket sale."},{"title":"A transaction alone is not enough","body":["Put the read and write inside <code>BEGIN … COMMIT</code> so they happen together or not at all. But that alone may not prevent two bookings. At the common <b>read committed</b> isolation level, both transactions can read the latest confirmed value, which is still 1 before either commits. <span class=\"term\">Isolation</span> controls what concurrent transactions can see of each other."],"callout":"ACID’s “I” has different levels. Check your database’s default: PostgreSQL and SQL Server use read committed; MySQL uses repeatable read."},{"title":"Lock the row while you decide","body":["Read the seat with <code>SELECT … FOR UPDATE</code>. Ana’s transaction locks row 7. When Ben tries to lock the same row, the database makes him wait. After Ana commits, Ben reads the updated value, 0, and tells the user the seat is sold out."],"callout":"A lock stays in place until the transaction commits. Keep transactions short; do not wait for a user or a payment service while holding a lock."},{"title":"Check and change in one statement","body":["Often the database can check and update the value in one atomic operation: <code>UPDATE seats SET left = left − 1 WHERE id = 7 AND left > 0</code>. Then check how many rows changed. One means the user got the seat; zero means it was already gone."],"callout":"A single conditional update is often a safe way to change counters, stock, or balances. There is no gap between checking and changing."},{"title":"Use a version number instead of a lock","body":["Locks are <em>pessimistic</em>: they assume a conflict may happen, so other requests wait. <span class=\"term\">Optimistic concurrency</span> assumes conflicts are rare. Read the row and its version, then write with <code>… WHERE id = 7 AND version = 8</code>, changing the version to 9. If another request changed it first, no row matches. Reload the new value and try again. Other users do not have to wait for a lock."],"callout":"This works well when someone edits a document, profile, or cart over time. Holding a lock for minutes would block other users."},{"title":"Choose an isolation level","body":["Databases offer several <span class=\"term\">isolation levels</span>. Stronger levels prevent more unexpected results, but may make requests wait or abort. <table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Prevents</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Read committed</b></td><td>Dirty reads</td><td>Lowest</td></tr><tr><td><b>Snapshot / repeatable read</b></td><td>Changing reads; lost updates in some databases<sup>1</sup></td><td>Some aborts</td></tr><tr><td><b>Serializable</b></td><td>Anomalies, as if transactions ran one at a time</td><td>More aborts and retries</td></tr></tbody></table><small><sup>1</sup> PostgreSQL aborts the second writer; MySQL repeatable read may allow the lost update. Database versions with the same level name can behave differently, so check the documentation.</small>"],"callout":"At serializable isolation, a database may abort a transaction instead of allowing an unsafe result. The application must catch the error and retry."},{"title":"Locking a missing row does not work","body":["A new booking flow checks the bookings table: “Is seat 7 booked? No—insert a row.” Both transactions use <code>FOR UPDATE</code>, but there is no row to lock yet. Both see nothing and both insert, so the seat is double-booked. A row that appears after the check is a <span class=\"term\">phantom</span>. <span class=\"term\">Write skew</span> is when concurrent transactions make decisions from what they read, then write changes that together break a rule."],"callout":"Possible fixes are serializable isolation, locking an existing parent row such as the seat, or adding a unique constraint."},{"title":"Two requests can deadlock","body":["Ana transfers money from account A to B. She locks A, then asks to lock B. At the same time, Ben transfers from B to A. He locks B, then asks for A. Each is waiting for the other, so neither can continue. This is a <span class=\"term\">deadlock</span>. The database detects the cycle and aborts one transaction; that request can then retry."],"callout":"Take locks in the same order, such as lowest account ID first. Then the requests cannot wait on each other in a cycle."},{"title":"Let the database enforce the rule","body":["Application code changes, and a future booking path may forget to lock the seat. Let the database enforce the rule with a <span class=\"term\">unique constraint</span> on <code>(concert, seat)</code>. It rejects a second booking for the same seat with a duplicate-key error, regardless of which code path tried it."],"callout":"Use constraints for important rules: unique values, valid foreign keys, or checks such as balance ≥ 0. They still apply when application code has a bug."},{"title":"Do not hold a lock during payment","body":["To avoid a double booking, someone puts the card payment inside the booking transaction: lock seat 7, call the payment provider, then commit. The provider takes three seconds. During that time the seat stays locked, other buyers wait, and each waiting request holds a database connection. On a busy sale, the connection pool can run out and the whole site can stall."],"callout":"Do not wait for a slow service, user, or email while holding a database lock or an open transaction."},{"title":"Use an expiring hold","body":["Split the booking into short transactions. First, mark seat 7 as <b>held for Ana until 12:10</b> and commit. Ana pays outside the transaction. Then a second transaction marks the seat <b>sold</b>. If she does not pay, a cleanup job releases the seat after the hold expires. Ben learns at once that the seat is taken; no lock is held while Ana pays."],"callout":"Ticket and airline sites often reserve a seat for a few minutes. An expiring hold replaces a long-running database lock."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Reading a value and then writing later can cause races when requests overlap.</li><li>Use <b>row locks</b>, one <b>atomic update</b>, or a <b>version check</b>.</li><li>Isolation levels trade stronger guarantees for waiting and retries.</li><li>Take locks in a consistent order and back important rules with <b>constraints</b>.</li><li>Keep transactions <b>short</b>. Use expiring holds instead of locking while waiting for payment.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Files &amp; Media</b> — where photos and videos go instead of the database, and how they move through the system."}]},"files":{"summary":"Store large files outside the database, then upload, process, and serve them safely.","goals":["Keep file bytes in object storage and file details in the database","Upload directly with presigned URLs and split large files into parts","Process files and serve them through a CDN","Keep file records and stored objects in sync","Use storage classes, lifecycle rules, and private buckets"],"steps":[{"title":"Start with photos in the database","body":["PhotoShare first saves each uploaded photo as a <span class=\"term\">BLOB</span> (binary data) in the database, beside its caption, owner, and date. This gives the team one place to store and back up everything. For the first few thousand users, it can work well."],"callout":"A photo may be several megabytes. Its details—owner, size, caption, and date—may use only a few hundred bytes."},{"title":"The database grows quickly","body":["Now a million photos arrive each day at 2 MB each: 2 TB a day, or about 730 TB in a year. The database, its replicas, and backups all grow. Restoring it can take days, and each new replica must copy all the files. Photo views also travel through app servers and the database. Large files can push useful rows out of database memory and slow queries for captions and likes."],"callout":"Databases are designed to search and update records. Sending megabytes of file data through them adds work."},{"title":"Store files separately from their details","body":["Move file bytes to <span class=\"term\">object storage</span>, such as Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage, or Azure Blob Storage. Store each file under a key, such as <code>photos/9f2c.jpg</code>. The app uses <code>PUT</code> to save it and <code>GET</code> to retrieve it. Object storage does not provide database queries or joins, but it can hold very large collections of files and costs less per terabyte than database disks. Providers still set limits for individual objects. Keep a small database row for each photo’s key, owner, size, and file type."],"callout":"A common design keeps file details in a database and the file bytes in object storage, connected by the object key."},{"title":"Do not relay large uploads through app servers","body":["If uploads pass through the app servers on their way to storage, a 2 GB video from a phone can hold a server connection and memory open for minutes. The servers must then be sized for network traffic, not just application work. A burst of video uploads can slow page loads for everyone."],"callout":"If a server only passes along bytes without inspecting them, it may be an expensive middle step."},{"title":"Upload directly to storage","body":["The app can give the phone temporary permission to upload directly. The phone asks to upload a 2 GB video. The app checks the user, creates a <code>pending</code> database row, and returns a <span class=\"term\">presigned URL</span>: a link signed by the app that allows one upload to one key for 15 minutes. The phone sends the file directly to object storage. When the upload finishes, storage sends an event and the app changes the row to <code>uploaded</code>."],"callout":"The signed URL can limit file type and size, so it cannot be reused to upload arbitrary files to your bucket."},{"title":"Send large files in parts","body":["A 2 GB upload over a phone connection may fail before it finishes. A <span class=\"term\">multipart upload</span> splits the file into smaller parts, such as 10 MB each. The phone sends the parts separately, often in parallel, using temporary signed links. If one part fails, only that part needs to be sent again. If the connection drops, the upload can resume from the parts already received. When all parts arrive, storage joins them into one file."],"callout":"Parallel parts can also use a network link better. Automatically remove abandoned uploads so their stored parts do not keep costing money."},{"title":"Prepare files for people to use","body":["An uploaded original is rarely the only version an app needs. Put each “upload complete” event on a queue (see <em>Message Queues</em>). Workers create thumbnails and photo sizes, scan for malware, and remove GPS location from photo metadata before sharing. They can also convert videos into several resolutions. When finished, a worker saves the new files and marks the row <code>ready</code>. Before that, the app can show “processing…”."],"callout":"Do not trust the file type or size reported by the phone. Workers should check the actual file and its contents."},{"title":"Serve downloads from a CDN","body":["People download photos far more often than they upload them, so downloads should not pass through app servers either. Put a <b>CDN</b> in front of object storage. The first viewer in a city fetches a copy from storage to a nearby edge server; later viewers in that city get the copy from the edge. Give each version a different key, such as <code>photos/9f2c_w640.webp</code>, so it can be cached for a year without serving an old version."],"callout":"For private photos, the app can issue a short-lived signed CDN URL. This is like a presigned upload URL, but it grants temporary permission to download."},{"title":"Let the video player adjust quality","body":["A video is too large to download before playback, and a viewer’s network speed can change. Split the video into segments a few seconds long. Encode each segment at several qualities, from 240p to 1080p, and create a small playlist that lists them. The player downloads segments from the CDN and picks a quality for the next one based on how quickly the last segment arrived. If a train enters a tunnel, the player can switch to 360p instead of freezing. This is <span class=\"term\">adaptive bitrate streaming</span>, such as HLS or DASH."],"callout":"Short segments can be cached separately at CDN edges, which helps popular videos load quickly."},{"title":"Keep file rows and objects in sync","body":["The database and object storage can disagree. A phone may lose its connection after getting a presigned URL, leaving a <code>pending</code> row forever. Or deleting a photo may remove its database row but leave its files stored and reachable. Use a status on each row. Storage events advance the status; deletes remove the row first and queue file deletion. A nightly <b>cleanup job</b> can remove pending rows older than a day and files that no row refers to."],"callout":"Data deletion may be required by laws that apply to your service. Make sure deletion reaches every copy, including resized files, video versions, backups, and CDN caches."},{"title":"Move old files to cheaper storage","body":["Many photos are viewed often during their first few weeks, then rarely. Object stores offer <b>storage classes</b>: standard storage for frequently read files, lower-cost options for infrequent reads, and archive options that cost much less but may charge more or take minutes to hours to retrieve. A <span class=\"term\">lifecycle rule</span> can move original photos to an archive tier after 90 days while keeping the smaller versions people still browse in standard storage."],"callout":"Cheap storage can have expensive or slow reads. Check how often files are actually accessed before archiving them."},{"title":"Keep the storage bucket private","body":["A developer makes the bucket public so images will display. Later, someone discovers they can list and download every photo, including private images and originals that contain location data. Keep buckets private and block public access at the account level. Serve files through a CDN with signed URLs. Give each service a key that can access only its own paths. Use hard-to-guess object names as an extra layer, not the only protection."],"callout":"A random key is not access control. If a URL is enough to read a private file, make the URL expire."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Keep file <b>bytes</b> in object storage and their <b>details</b> in the database, joined by an object key.</li><li>Let phones upload directly with <b>presigned URLs</b>. Send large files in parts that can be retried or resumed.</li><li><b>Workers</b> create thumbnails and video versions, and remove data that should not be shared.</li><li>Serve files through a <b>CDN</b> with versioned keys; stream video in adaptive segments.</li><li>Keep database rows and files in sync, move old files to cheaper storage, and keep buckets private.</li></ul>"],"callout":"That completes Data &amp; Storage. Next: <b>Communication</b> — how services talk to each other."}]},"apis":{"summary":"Compare REST, GraphQL, and RPC: three ways for programs to request data and actions.","goals":["What an API contract promises","How REST resources and HTTP verbs work","How GraphQL requests only selected fields","How services use RPC to call each other","How to page through lists and change APIs safely"],"steps":[{"title":"Programs need an agreement","body":["A mobile app shows a profile with a name, avatar, recent posts, and follower count. That data is on the server, so the app needs a way to request it. An <span class=\"term\">API</span> is the agreed contract: which addresses exist, what the app sends, and what the server returns. Here the app sends JSON over HTTPS."],"callout":"An API is a promise to its users. Once apps depend on it, changes must avoid breaking them."},{"title":"REST: resources and HTTP actions","body":["<span class=\"term\">REST</span> organizes data as <b>resources</b> with addresses, such as <code>/users/42</code> or <code>/users/42/posts</code>. HTTP verbs say what to do: <code>GET</code> reads, <code>POST</code> creates, <code>PUT</code> or <code>PATCH</code> updates, and <code>DELETE</code> removes. This style is simple and works with many tools. Browsers and CDNs can cache many <code>GET</code> responses. <code>GET</code>, <code>PUT</code>, and <code>DELETE</code> are usually <span class=\"term\">idempotent</span>: repeating them has the same result as doing them once. <code>POST</code> may create another item each time, which matters when retrying."],"callout":"HTTP status codes describe the result: 200 OK, 201 Created, 404 Not Found, 429 Too Many Requests, or 500 server error."},{"title":"Too many calls and too much data","body":["On a phone, each network round trip may take about 100 ms. The app makes three requests one after another. Some requests may need an ID from an earlier response, so they cannot run at the same time. Each API response also returns a whole object: 48 KB arrive even though the screen needs about 3 KB. This is <span class=\"term\">over-fetching</span>. Needing several requests to get the data for one screen is <span class=\"term\">under-fetching</span>."],"callout":"Extra round trips and unused data hurt most on slow networks, which is common for mobile users."},{"title":"GraphQL asks for selected fields","body":["With <span class=\"term\">GraphQL</span>, the app sends one query describing the fields it wants. A GraphQL server fetches those fields from the services behind it and returns them in one response. In the diagram, one request goes from the phone, splits between Users and Posts services, then returns as one response."],"callout":"GraphQL can help when iOS, Android, web, or TV clients need different fields from the same backend."},{"title":"Flexible queries need limits","body":["Clients can ask for any data allowed by the schema, including followers of followers and all of their posts. A deeply nested request can overload the Users service. GraphQL queries are often sent as <code>POST</code> requests to one URL, so ordinary HTTP caches and CDNs cannot cache them without extra work, such as persisted queries."],"callout":"Production GraphQL APIs should limit query depth and cost. They may also allow only approved, or “persisted,” queries."},{"title":"Call internal services with RPC","body":["Services inside a data center often call one another using <span class=\"term\">RPC</span> (remote procedure call). The caller invokes a function on another machine, much like calling a local function. A common option is <b>gRPC</b>. Both sides use a typed schema, often Protocol Buffers, and generated code. The messages are compact binary data sent over HTTP/2."],"callout":"gRPC is fast, typed, and supports streaming. Browsers usually need a gRPC-Web proxy, so it is more common between internal services."},{"title":"Split a long list into pages","body":["A celebrity may have two million followers. Returning the whole list would take hundreds of megabytes and a long time. APIs split long lists into <b>pages</b>. An offset request such as <code>?offset=40000&amp;limit=20</code> is easy to read, but the database may still skip 40,000 rows. A <span class=\"term\">cursor</span> can say “return the 20 entries after ID 9f3c.” An index can jump to that point, and new items are less likely to make the list skip or repeat entries."],"callout":"Return the cursor for the next page. The app sends it back in its next request."},{"title":"Keep old apps working","body":["A code cleanup renames <code>name</code> to <code>display_name</code>. The new app works, but many phones still use an older version and their profile screens now fail. You cannot force every phone to update. Evolve APIs by adding the new field and keeping the old one until it is no longer used. For a breaking change, publish a new version such as <code>/v2/</code> and run both versions."],"callout":"Every public API field is a promise. Measure which clients still use a field before removing or renaming it."},{"title":"Choose the API style","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Best for</th><th>Watch out</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>REST</b></td><td>Public APIs, simple read, create, update, and delete operations; caching</td><td>May need many calls per screen</td></tr><tr><td><b>GraphQL</b></td><td>Many clients with different screens</td><td>Expensive queries and caching</td></tr><tr><td><b>gRPC</b></td><td>Calls between services</td><td>Browsers need a proxy</td></tr></tbody></table>Systems can mix styles: use REST or GraphQL for client apps and gRPC between internal services."],"callout":"Whichever style you use, add fields instead of changing or removing fields that existing clients may depend on."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>An <b>API</b> is an agreement between programs.</li><li><b>REST</b> uses resources and HTTP verbs. It is simple and cacheable, but a screen may need several calls.</li><li><b>GraphQL</b> lets clients request selected fields in one query; limit expensive queries.</li><li><b>gRPC</b> provides fast, typed calls between services.</li><li>Use <b>cursors</b> for long lists and evolve APIs without breaking old clients.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Message Queues</b> — handle work without making the user wait for it."}]},"queues":{"summary":"Answer quickly, then process slow work in the background without losing jobs.","goals":["Why slow work should happen outside a user request","How producers, queues, and consumers work together","How queues absorb traffic spikes","How acknowledgements, retries, and dead-letter queues work","How to keep order and apply backpressure"],"steps":[{"title":"Slow work blocks the request","body":["Creators upload videos. Each upload needs about 30 seconds of work to convert it into several sizes. Right now the web server does that work before answering the upload request. The creator waits, requests time out, and video processing slows down other pages too."],"callout":"Slow, heavy, or unreliable work inside a request—such as video conversion, email, reports, or a third-party call—makes the user wait."},{"title":"Put a queue between the request and the work","body":["The web server saves the upload, puts a small <b>job</b> message on a <span class=\"term\">message queue</span>, then replies: “processing—we’ll notify you.” Separate <span class=\"term\">worker</span> machines take jobs from the queue and convert the videos. The web server is the <b>producer</b>; the workers are <b>consumers</b>."],"callout":"Popular options include RabbitMQ, Amazon SQS, Google Pub/Sub, and Kafka, a retained event log that can also be used as a queue."},{"title":"Let a queue absorb a spike","body":["A popular creator goes live and uploads increase tenfold. Without a queue, the spike could overload the web servers. With a queue, web servers stay quick because they only add jobs. The jobs wait in line while workers continue at a steady pace, then catch up after the rush."],"callout":"A queue turns “too much work right now” into “some users wait longer for the result.” This is called load leveling."},{"title":"Add workers to catch up faster","body":["To finish the backlog sooner, start more workers. Each job goes to one worker, not all of them. This is the <span class=\"term\">competing consumers</span> pattern. Workers can be added easily because they are stateless. Teams may automatically add workers when the queue grows."],"callout":"Scale producers and consumers separately: add web servers for more requests and workers for more processing."},{"title":"A worker can fail before it finishes","body":["A worker crashes halfway through a video. If the queue deleted the job as soon as it handed it out, the work would be lost and the creator might wait forever. Workers can crash or be replaced, so the queue must allow for failures."],"callout":"Handing a job to a worker does not mean the worker has finished it."},{"title":"Acknowledge only after finishing","body":["Keep a job hidden while a worker is processing it. When the work is done, the worker sends an <span class=\"term\">acknowledgement</span> (ack), and the queue deletes the job. If no ack arrives before a timeout, the job appears again for another worker. This means a job may run twice—for example, if a worker finishes but crashes before sending its ack. Make jobs <span class=\"term\">idempotent</span>, so repeating one does not change the result twice."],"callout":"This is <b>at-least-once delivery</b>: a job is retried until acknowledged, but duplicates are possible. Exactly-once behavior is difficult; idempotent workers help produce the same result."},{"title":"Move repeated failures aside","body":["One upload is corrupt, so every worker that tries it fails. The queue retries the same job again and again, using workers and blocking other work. Count attempts and move a job to a <span class=\"term\">dead-letter queue</span> (DLQ) after, for example, three failures. A person or script can inspect it there."],"callout":"Alert when the dead-letter queue grows. It can be an early sign of a bug or bad input."},{"title":"Events may finish out of order","body":["An account has events named <code>created</code>, <code>email changed</code>, and <code>deleted</code>. Several workers process them in parallel. A slow worker may finish “email changed” after another worker has already handled “deleted.” Many queues do not promise order, and parallel workers can change completion order even if messages were read in order."],"callout":"If correctness depends on order, design and enforce that order explicitly."},{"title":"Keep order per key with partitions","body":["Systems such as Kafka divide a topic into <span class=\"term\">partitions</span>. Messages with the same key, such as an account ID, go to the same partition. One worker reads each partition in order. Events for one account stay in order, while different accounts can be handled in parallel by other partitions."],"callout":"Order is guaranteed per key, not across every message. Global order usually means one consumer and no parallel processing."},{"title":"A queue cannot create more capacity","body":["A queue can absorb a spike, but if jobs arrive faster than workers finish them for hours, the backlog keeps growing and can fill storage. Watch the <b>age</b> of the oldest job, not just the queue length. Add workers, cap the queue, or ask producers to slow down or reject new jobs with a clear “try later.”"],"callout":"A queue buys time; it does not add capacity. Over time, workers must process jobs at least as fast as they arrive."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Move slow work out of the request: add a job to a <b>queue</b> and answer quickly.</li><li>Queues absorb spikes; add <b>workers</b> to reduce the backlog.</li><li><b>Acknowledge</b> after work is done. Retries can create duplicates, so make jobs idempotent.</li><li>Move repeatedly failing jobs to a <b>dead-letter queue</b>.</li><li>Use partitions to keep order per key, watch queue age, and push back when needed.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Pub/Sub &amp; Events</b> — when one event should notify several services."}]},"pubsub":{"summary":"Publish a fact once, then let interested services react without slowing the original request.","goals":["Why direct service calls create dependencies","How publishers, topics, and subscribers work","How a queue differs from a topic","What event-driven design costs","How an outbox and replay protect events"],"steps":[{"title":"Checkout calls several services","body":["When a customer places an order, four things must happen: send a confirmation email, reserve stock, record analytics, and add loyalty points. The order service calls each one and waits. The shopper waits for all four responses, almost a second."],"callout":"Every new feature that reacts to an order requires changes, tests, and a redeploy of the order service."},{"title":"An email outage stops checkout","body":["The email provider is down. The order service calls it, gets an error, and checkout fails because it waits for every service. An email that could be sent ten minutes later has stopped all sales."],"callout":"If checkout depends on four services, each one’s availability affects checkout. Four services at 99.9% availability give about 99.6% combined availability in this example."},{"title":"Publish an event instead","body":["The order service saves the order and publishes one <code>OrderPlaced</code> event to a topic on an <span class=\"term\">event bus</span>. It can then answer the shopper. Email, inventory, analytics, and loyalty services <b>subscribe</b> to that topic and each receive a copy of the event."],"callout":"Common event brokers include Kafka, Amazon SNS with SQS, Google Pub/Sub, RabbitMQ fanout exchanges, and Redis Streams."},{"title":"Queue or topic?","body":["In the previous lesson, each job went to one worker; a queue shares work among workers. A topic does the opposite: it sends each event to every subscriber. Systems often combine them. Each subscribing service gets its own queue, which can be shared by several copies of that service."],"callout":"A queue says “someone do this work.” A topic says “this happened.”"},{"title":"Add a subscriber without changing checkout","body":["The risk team wants to check each order for fraud. It builds a service that subscribes to <code>OrderPlaced</code>. The order service does not need to change or redeploy, and it does not need to know about fraud checks. This is <span class=\"term\">loose coupling</span>: the publisher and subscribers do not depend directly on each other."],"callout":"Teams can add a new reaction to an event without coordinating a release with the publisher."},{"title":"A subscriber can catch up later","body":["The email service is down again, but checkout still works. The event bus holds email’s messages until it recovers. Then the email service works through its backlog and sends the confirmations late. The outage affected email processing, not checkout."],"callout":"Durable events let a slow or unavailable subscriber catch up when it returns."},{"title":"Events add their own costs","body":["Events separate services, but the workflow is now spread across several codebases. Subscribers act later, so data may be briefly inconsistent. Events may arrive twice or out of order, and debugging requires tracing them across services."],"callout":"Name events as facts that already happened, such as OrderPlaced or PaymentFailed. Make subscribers safe to run more than once."},{"title":"Save the order and its event together","body":["The order service saves an order, then publishes <code>OrderPlaced</code>. If it crashes between those actions, the order exists but no event was sent. The <span class=\"term\">transactional outbox</span> pattern fixes this: save the order and an event row in an <code>outbox</code> table in the same database transaction. A relay later publishes every outbox row to the event bus."],"callout":"Either the order and its event are both saved, or neither is. The relay retries until the bus accepts an event, so subscribers must handle duplicates. The relay can poll the table or read the database log using <b>change data capture</b>, such as Debezium."},{"title":"Replay retained events","body":["Analytics finds a bug that miscounted revenue for the last 30 days. A basic queue may delete each event after it is consumed. A log-based bus such as Kafka can retain events for days or weeks. Analytics fixes its code, moves its position back 30 days, and processes the events again without affecting other subscribers."],"callout":"A new subscriber can also read retained events to build its data from history instead of starting empty."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Direct calls can make a caller slow and fragile, and connect teams too tightly.</li><li><b>Publish</b> an event; each <b>subscriber</b> gets its own copy.</li><li>New services can subscribe later, and unavailable subscribers can catch up.</li><li>Expect delays, duplicates, and harder tracing.</li><li>Use an <b>outbox</b> so database changes and events stay together; retained logs allow replay.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Real-time Updates</b> — how events reach a user’s screen."}]},"realtime":{"summary":"Compare ways to send chat, scores, and notifications to users as events happen.","goals":["Why ordinary HTTP does not let a server push first","How polling and long polling compare","How SSE and WebSockets send updates","How many persistent connections scale","How offline users catch up and show presence"],"steps":[{"title":"Bob sends a message. How does Alice hear?","body":["Bob’s message reaches the chat server, and Alice’s phone should show it quickly. With ordinary HTTP, each exchange starts with a <b>client request</b> and a server response. The server cannot start a new request to Alice’s phone just to say “you have a message.”"],"callout":"Phones and browsers are usually behind home routers or mobile networks that block incoming connections, so the client must connect out first."},{"title":"Polling: ask again and again","body":["The simplest option is for Alice’s app to ask “anything new?” every two seconds. Most answers say no; occasionally one includes a new message. This uses ordinary HTTP requests, but a message may be up to two seconds late and many requests return nothing."],"callout":"Polling can work for information that changes slowly, such as a build status or order tracking page. Choose a sensible interval."},{"title":"A million phones create many requests","body":["One million users polling every two seconds generate 500,000 requests per second. About 95% may return “nothing new.” Many servers are busy answering requests that carry no update. Polling more often reduces delay but increases the number of requests."],"callout":"Polling trades freshness for server load: cut the interval in half and the request rate doubles."},{"title":"Long polling: wait for news","body":["With <span class=\"term\">long polling</span>, the app sends a request and the server holds it open until a message arrives or about 30 seconds pass. Then the server responds and the app immediately asks again. Messages can arrive quickly, and there are fewer empty responses."],"callout":"Long polling uses ordinary HTTP, so it can be a fallback when a better option is unavailable."},{"title":"SSE: keep one stream open","body":["With <span class=\"term\">Server-Sent Events</span> (SSE), the app opens one request and the server keeps the response open. It sends each new event down that stream. SSE is one-way, from server to client, and browsers reconnect automatically. Alice sends her own messages using normal requests."],"callout":"SSE works well for feeds, notifications, live scores, dashboards, and streaming AI responses."},{"title":"WebSockets: send in either direction","body":["A <span class=\"term\">WebSocket</span> begins as an HTTP request and then upgrades to a persistent two-way connection. Either side can send a message at any time, with little overhead per message. Bob and Alice each keep a socket open, and messages pass through the server in a few milliseconds."],"callout":"Use WebSockets when the client also sends frequent updates, such as in chat, multiplayer games, collaborative editing, or trading screens."},{"title":"Find which server holds Alice’s connection","body":["Each open connection uses memory, so a million users need many chat servers. Bob’s socket is on server 2, but Alice’s is on server 1. Server 2 cannot send to Alice directly. The servers use <span class=\"term\">pub/sub</span>: server 2 publishes to Alice’s channel, and the server holding her socket receives the event and forwards it."],"callout":"Pub/sub lets real-time servers relay messages to the server that owns a user’s connection."},{"title":"Store messages for offline users","body":["Alice enters the subway and her socket disconnects. Bob’s message has nowhere to go. Save each message first, then push it if the recipient is connected. When Alice reconnects, her app says “I last saw message 1041” and fetches everything after it. If she stays offline, a mobile push notification service such as APNs or FCM can wake her phone."],"callout":"Real-time delivery is a shortcut. The stored message is the source of truth and lets a user catch up after reconnecting."},{"title":"Track who seems to be online","body":["A green dot next to a name needs <span class=\"term\">presence</span> information. Each app sends a small <b>heartbeat</b> every 30 seconds; the server updates the user’s “last seen” time. If no heartbeat arrives for a minute, mark them offline. With a million users, that is about 33,000 heartbeats each second. Keep presence in a fast store with expiring keys, and send changes only to people viewing that contact."],"callout":"Presence is approximate on purpose. A few seconds of delay is usually acceptable and saves a lot of traffic."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Delay</th><th>Direction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Polling</b></td><td>Up to the interval</td><td>Client asks</td></tr><tr><td><b>Long polling</b></td><td>Low</td><td>Client asks; server waits</td></tr><tr><td><b>SSE</b></td><td>Low</td><td>Server → client</td></tr><tr><td><b>WebSocket</b></td><td>Lowest</td><td>Both ways</td></tr></tbody></table>Persistent connections need many servers at scale, joined by <b>pub/sub</b>. Store each message first so offline users can catch up."],"callout":"That completes Communication. Next: <b>Reliability</b> — starting with redundancy and failover."}]},"redundancy":{"summary":"Plan for failures so one broken component does not take the whole service offline.","goals":["How availability is measured in nines","How to find and remove single points of failure","Why copies should span failure domains","How RTO, RPO, and failover testing shape a design","How canary deploys and regions reduce risk"],"steps":[{"title":"How available is a chain of components?","body":["A simple service needs a load balancer, an app server, and a database. Each is available 99.9% of the time. A request needs all three, so their availabilities multiply: 0.999 × 0.999 × 0.999 ≈ 99.7%. That is about 26 hours of downtime per year."],"callout":"Availability is often described in “nines.” Each extra 9 reduces the allowed downtime by about ten times."},{"title":"Each component can be a single point of failure","body":["A <span class=\"term\">single point of failure</span> (SPOF) is one component whose failure can stop the service. Here, that includes each component in the chain. A failed disk, crashed operating system, or broken deployment can leave a million users with an error page."],"callout":"Hardware fails, software crashes, and people make mistakes. Plan for each kind of failure and decide what should keep working."},{"title":"Add a backup for each part","body":["Add another copy of each component: an active and standby load balancer, two app servers sharing traffic, and a database follower ready to take over. If both copies must fail for the service to stop, two independent 0.1% failure chances multiply to 0.0001%. In an ideal calculation, two 99.9% copies give 99.9999% availability."],"callout":"Real pairs do worse because failover takes time and copies may share a building, network, or deployment. This example uses a more cautious 99.95%."},{"title":"Copies can fail together","body":["All the copies are still in one data center. A power outage, flood, network cut, or cooling failure can take out the building and every copy inside it. These are <span class=\"term\">correlated failures</span>: one event affects several components. Redundancy helps most when copies fail independently."],"callout":"Shared power, networks, racks, software versions, or one bad configuration change can affect every copy."},{"title":"Spread copies across availability zones","body":["Cloud regions contain <span class=\"term\">availability zones</span>: separate data centers, often kilometres apart, with their own power and network and fast links between them. Put an app server and a database copy in each zone. A problem that affects one zone then takes out only part of the system."],"callout":"Spread copies across racks, zones, and, for the most critical systems, regions."},{"title":"Fail over when a zone goes dark","body":["Zone A loses power. Health checks fail, so the load balancer stops sending it traffic. The database follower in zone B becomes the leader. Users whose requests were in progress may see an error and retry. Within a minute, the service runs from zone B."],"callout":"Zone B needs enough spare capacity to handle all traffic by itself. A backup that cannot carry the load is not useful redundancy."},{"title":"Set recovery time and recovery point","body":["Two targets describe a failover. <span class=\"term\">RTO</span> (recovery time objective) is how long the service can take to return. <span class=\"term\">RPO</span> (recovery point objective) is how much recent data the service can afford to lose. With asynchronous replication, a follower can be seconds behind, so recent writes may be missing. Synchronous replication can make RPO zero, but slows writes."],"callout":"Agree on RTO and RPO with the business. Those targets shape the design and its cost."},{"title":"Test failover before a real outage","body":["A failover plan that is never tested can quietly break: configuration can drift, a backup may be too small, or a script may contain a typo. Teams can deliberately test failures in production during planned hours. This practice is called <span class=\"term\">chaos engineering</span>. Here, a test stops one app server while the team watches the service respond."],"callout":"Netflix’s Chaos Monkey randomly stops servers. Start smaller with a planned game day and one failure at a time."},{"title":"A bad release can break every copy","body":["A release with a bug goes to every app server in both zones at once. They all fail on the same request. Redundant machines do not help when every copy runs the same broken code. Many outages come from changes—code, configuration, or infrastructure—not hardware failure."],"callout":"Identical copies can fail in the same way. A bad change can create a correlated failure."},{"title":"Release to a canary first","body":["Roll out the change in stages. First send version 2.4 to one server or 1% of traffic. This small test group is a <span class=\"term\">canary</span>. Compare its error rate and latency with the other servers. If it looks worse, roll back while only a small share of users was affected. If it looks good, continue to 10%, 50%, and then 100%. Teams can also deploy one zone at a time."],"callout":"Feature flags help too: deploy code while a feature is off, then turn it on gradually and turn it off quickly if problems appear."},{"title":"Use more than one region","body":["Availability zones protect against one data center failing, but a whole <b>region</b> can still go down. A second region can also serve users on another continent. A global DNS or anycast front door can direct users to a nearby healthy region. The data is harder: copies across an ocean may lag by about 100 ms."],"callout":"Multi-region systems cost more and are harder to run. Many services are well served by multiple zones in one region and reliable backups."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Availability in a chain is the product of each required component’s availability.</li><li>Remove <b>single points of failure</b> with backup copies.</li><li>Copies should fail independently, so spread them across zones.</li><li>Set <b>RTO</b> and <b>RPO</b>, then test failover.</li><li>Many outages come from changes; release with <b>canaries</b>.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Rate Limiting</b> — protect the service from too much traffic."}]},"rate-limiting":{"summary":"Give each client a fair request budget so one runaway script cannot crowd out everyone.","goals":["How one client can use shared capacity","How token buckets and other limits work","How gateways share a client’s limit","How 429 responses and tenant quotas help","How to drop low-priority work during overload"],"steps":[{"title":"One client can use all the capacity","body":["A customer’s script has a bug and calls your API in a tight loop: 5,000 requests every second. The API servers spend nearly all their time on this script. Real users wait behind it and time out. Nobody attacked the service; one client is simply using more than its share."],"callout":"Shared capacity needs a fairness rule. Without one, the loudest client can use it all."},{"title":"Limit requests at the front door","body":["Put a <span class=\"term\">rate limiter</span> in the gateway before the API. Identify each client by API key, user ID, or IP address, and give it a budget such as 10 requests per second. Allow requests within the budget. Reject extra requests right away with <code>429 Too Many Requests</code>, before they use API resources."],"callout":"An early rejection is cheap: the gateway answers in microseconds, and the API never handles the extra request."},{"title":"Use a token bucket","body":["A common method is the <span class=\"term\">token bucket</span>. Each client has a bucket that holds up to 10 tokens. The bucket refills at 10 tokens per second. Each request uses one token; if none remain, the gateway returns 429. A real user’s bucket may stay mostly full, while a fast script empties its bucket and gets limited."],"callout":"The bucket size allows a short burst. The refill rate limits the average over time."},{"title":"Compare counting methods","body":["A <b>fixed window</b> counts requests in each calendar minute and resets at :00. A client can use its full budget at 12:00:59, then use it again at 12:01:00—twice the limit in two seconds. A <b>sliding window</b> smooths that boundary. A token bucket allows a controlled burst. Many API gateways use a sliding window or token bucket."],"callout":"If you need a steady outflow to a fragile partner API, a <b>leaky bucket</b> can queue requests and release them at a fixed pace."},{"title":"Share one limit across gateways","body":["A service may need several gateways. If each keeps a separate counter, a client spread across 10 gateways could get 10 times its limit. Keep the counter in a shared fast store such as Redis. Each gateway atomically increases the counter and checks the total."],"callout":"A shared store adds about 1 ms per request. Some systems trade exact counts for speed by syncing local counters once a second."},{"title":"Tell clients when to try again","body":["A useful 429 response tells the client when to return. Include a <code>Retry-After: 30</code> header and headers showing the limit and remaining requests. A well-behaved client waits before retrying. The script owner can fix the loop so it uses its fair share."],"callout":"Publish limits in the API documentation. Offer higher limits to customers who need them."},{"title":"Limit traffic per customer too","body":["One company uses your API through 10,000 users for a nightly import. Each person stays within their own limit, but together they use 70% of your capacity. Other customers slow down. A per-user limit cannot catch this. Add a limit for each <b>tenant</b> (customer account), based on its plan, as well as per-user limits."],"callout":"Limits can be layered by user, customer, endpoint, and the whole system. Expensive endpoints can have lower limits."},{"title":"Drop low-priority work first","body":["Rate limits stop one client from using too much capacity. On launch day, all users may arrive at once and total demand can still exceed what the system serves. <span class=\"term\">Load shedding</span> means deliberately dropping lower-priority requests first. The gateway can reject background work such as prefetching, recommendations, and analytics so logins and checkouts continue working."],"callout":"Failing some requests on purpose can be better than letting every request fail by accident."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Give each client a <b>budget</b> and reject excess requests early with <b>429</b>.</li><li>A <b>token bucket</b> allows short bursts but limits the average rate.</li><li>Use a <b>shared store</b> to coordinate limits across gateways.</li><li>Set limits per user, customer, endpoint, and system.</li><li>During overload, <b>shed</b> low-priority work first.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Timeouts &amp; Retries</b> — what to do when a service you call is struggling."}]},"timeouts-retries":{"summary":"Call struggling services safely: stop waiting in time, retry carefully, and know when to stop.","goals":["How one slow service can spread failure","How timeouts and fallbacks protect callers","How retries can cause storms","How backoff, jitter, and circuit breakers help","How idempotency, deadlines, and bulkheads limit damage"],"steps":[{"title":"A slow service can stall its caller","body":["The payment service used to answer in 50 ms but now takes 30 seconds. The order service calls it and waits without a limit. Each waiting call holds a thread. Soon all 200 threads are stuck, and the order service cannot even serve pages that do not need payments. One slow dependency has caused a wider outage."],"callout":"This is a <span class=\"term\">cascading failure</span>: a problem spreads to other services that wait without limits."},{"title":"Set a timeout and a fallback","body":["Give every outgoing call a <span class=\"term\">timeout</span>. If payments has not answered in 800 ms, stop waiting and free the thread. Then use a fallback: accept the order as “payment pending” and charge it shortly afterward. The order service can keep serving other pages."],"callout":"Choose timeouts based on real response times: a little above the dependency’s p99, and shorter than the timeout set by the caller above you."},{"title":"Retry brief failures","body":["Some failures are temporary: a dropped network packet, a restarting server, or a brief disruption. Trying once more a moment later may succeed. In this example, one retry turns many temporary failures into successful checkouts."],"callout":"Retry errors that may work next time, such as a timeout or 503. Retrying a 400 Bad Request usually repeats the same failure."},{"title":"Retries can overwhelm a service","body":["Now payments is truly overloaded. Every caller retries three times right away. Each failed request can create four requests total, so payments gets four times its normal traffic. It cannot recover while retries keep arriving. A retry storm can make clients behave like a denial-of-service attack."],"callout":"Retries multiply across layers too. Three retries at each of three layers can create up to 64 calls for one user action."},{"title":"Wait longer and add randomness","body":["Wait before retrying, and increase the wait each time: 100 ms, then 200, then 400. This is <span class=\"term\">exponential backoff</span>. Add random <span class=\"term\">jitter</span> so many clients do not retry together. Limit the number of attempts and the share of traffic that may be retries—for example, a 10% retry budget. This gives payments time to recover."],"callout":"Cloud software libraries often include backoff and jitter. Check that it is enabled and that retries are not stacked at several layers."},{"title":"Stop calling a service that is down","body":["Payments goes fully down. Even with a timeout, every request wastes 800 ms finding out. A <span class=\"term\">circuit breaker</span> tracks failures. After a threshold it <b>opens</b> and fails calls quickly without contacting payments. Every 30 seconds it allows one test call through in the <b>half-open</b> state. If that call works, the breaker closes and normal traffic resumes."],"callout":"Like a circuit breaker in a home, it trips to protect the rest of the system and resets after the fault is fixed."},{"title":"Restore traffic gradually","body":["Payments recovers. The next test call succeeds, so the breaker closes. Traffic returns gradually instead of flooding the service as soon as it is back. Meanwhile, a queue charges orders that were marked “payment pending.” No order was lost, and other pages stayed available."],"callout":"Libraries such as Resilience4j and Polly, and service meshes such as Envoy or Istio, can provide timeouts, retries, and circuit breakers."},{"title":"Use an idempotency key","body":["Payments charges a card, but its response is lost on the way back. The order service times out and retries. Without protection, the customer is charged twice. Send a unique <span class=\"term\">idempotency key</span> for the order with every attempt. Payments remembers the result for each key and returns the same result for duplicates instead of charging again."],"callout":"Any operation that may be retried should be <b>idempotent</b>: repeating it has the same effect as doing it once."},{"title":"Pass a deadline through the call chain","body":["The shopper’s browser gives up after two seconds. If the order service has already spent 1.8 seconds, asking payments to start a fresh 800 ms of work is wasted; the user will never see the answer. Pass a <span class=\"term\">deadline</span> with each request, such as “answer by 12:00:02.000.” Each service checks the remaining time and passes it on. If no time remains, stop."],"callout":"gRPC can pass deadlines automatically. With HTTP, a service can pass the remaining time in a header."},{"title":"Give each dependency its own pool","body":["When payments was slow, it used all 200 order-service threads. The order service can give each dependency a separate pool—for example, 40 threads for payments, 40 for inventory, and the rest for other work. If payments slows down, only its 40 threads fill up. Browsing, search, and the cart can keep working. This is the <span class=\"term\">bulkhead</span> pattern, named after the partitions in a ship."],"callout":"Timeouts limit how long a call can hurt you. Bulkheads limit how many calls can."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Every remote call needs a <b>timeout</b> and a useful fallback.</li><li>Retry temporary failures with <b>exponential backoff</b>, <b>jitter</b>, and a limit.</li><li>A <b>circuit breaker</b> stops repeated calls to a failed service.</li><li>Make retried operations <b>idempotent</b>.</li><li>Pass <b>deadlines</b> and isolate dependencies with <b>bulkheads</b>.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Observability</b> — how to find out that payments is slow in the first place."}]},"observability":{"summary":"Use metrics, logs, and traces to find what is wrong, where it happens, and why.","goals":["Why “it is slow” is hard to diagnose","How RED metrics and dashboards narrow the search","How SLOs and error budgets guide alerts","How logs and traces tell a request’s story","How to respond to incidents and learn from them"],"steps":[{"title":"“Checkout is slow.” Where?","body":["Support tickets say checkout takes several seconds. A checkout request touches the gateway, orders, inventory, payments, a database, and a fraud-check service. The diagram shows the request waiting somewhere, but the team cannot see where. Every server’s CPU looks fine, so nobody knows where to start."],"callout":"If users find a problem before your team does, you are missing useful visibility into the system."},{"title":"Measure requests over time","body":["Each service records numbers about the requests it handles using the <span class=\"term\">RED method</span>: <b>Rate</b> (requests per second), <b>Errors</b> (share that failed), and <b>Duration</b> (how long they take, including percentiles). Send these measurements to a time-series database and dashboard. Now the team can see that inventory and the database are fast; payments is slow."],"callout":"Common tools include Prometheus and Grafana, Datadog, and CloudWatch. Watch p99 as well as the average. For machines, USE means utilization, saturation, and errors."},{"title":"Alert on user-facing problems","body":["An alert for “CPU above 80%” may fire often without showing that users have a problem. Alert on symptoms users notice. An <span class=\"term\">SLI</span> is a service-level indicator, such as the share of checkouts that finish in under one second. An <span class=\"term\">SLO</span> is the target for that indicator, such as 99.9%. An <span class=\"term\">error budget</span> is the amount of failure allowed by that target. Alert when the system is using that budget too quickly."],"callout":"An error budget gives reliability and feature work a shared measure. While the budget remains, teams can ship; when it is used up, they focus on reliability."},{"title":"Logs tell what happened","body":["Metrics show <em>where</em> a problem occurs; <span class=\"term\">logs</span> record <em>what happened</em>. Each service writes events with a timestamp. But at thousands of requests per second, lines from different requests mix together. Give each request an ID at the gateway, pass it through the services, and add it to every log line. Then filter by that ID to see one request’s story."],"callout":"Write structured logs, such as JSON, instead of unstructured text so you can filter by request ID, user, service, or status."},{"title":"Trace one request across services","body":["A <span class=\"term\">distributed trace</span> records each step, or <b>span</b>, of one request. A span has a start time and duration; spans share a trace ID. Shown as a timeline, the trace reveals where time was spent. Here, the request takes 2.4 seconds and waits 2.1 seconds for the external fraud service."],"callout":"OpenTelemetry can collect traces. Jaeger, Tempo, Honeycomb, and Datadog can help explore them. Instrument once and reuse the data."},{"title":"Fix the cause and check the result","body":["Now that the team knows the cause, it can set a 300 ms timeout for fraud checks, cache recent scores, and review rare slow requests later. The dashboards show whether it worked: p99 falls from 2.4 seconds to 310 ms, and the SLO alert clears. Finding the cause took minutes instead of hours."],"callout":"Metrics show that something is wrong, traces show where, and logs help explain why. You need all three."},{"title":"Respond to an error spike","body":["At 03:12, an alert shows checkout errors rising from 0.1% to 6%. The dashboard points to inventory. The deployment log shows a configuration change there at 03:10. First reduce the impact: roll back the change and restore service. Investigate the root cause after users can check out again."],"callout":"Show deployments and configuration changes on dashboards. “What changed?” is often the first incident question."},{"title":"Learn with a post-mortem","body":["After the incident, the team writes a <span class=\"term\">post-mortem</span>: what happened over time, the impact, the root cause, and actions to prevent a repeat. It is <b>blameless</b>: ask how the system allowed a mistake through, not who made it. Here, configuration changes will use the same canary rollout as code."],"callout":"Incidents can teach the team how to improve the system. Good post-mortems help prevent the same failure from happening again."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li><b>Metrics</b> such as rate, errors, and duration show where things are slow or failing.</li><li>Alert on <b>SLOs</b>, which reflect what users experience, not only raw CPU use.</li><li><b>Logs</b> with a request ID show what one request did.</li><li><b>Traces</b> follow a request across services and show where time went.</li><li>During an incident, <b>reduce the impact first</b>, then write a blameless post-mortem.</li></ul>Instrument before an outage; that is the wrong time to add logging."],"callout":"That completes Reliability. Next: <b>Architecture</b> — starting with monoliths and microservices."}]},"microservices":{"summary":"Compare one deployable application with separately owned services, and learn the costs of splitting.","goals":["Where a monolith works well and where it can hurt","How to split services by business capability","What network calls add","How local read copies replace cross-service queries","How contract tests and gradual migration help"],"steps":[{"title":"One application, one deployment","body":["Your shop is a <span class=\"term\">monolith</span>: one codebase contains accounts, catalog, orders, and payments. The whole app is deployed as one unit, with several copies behind a load balancer and one shared database. For a small team this is simple. Features call each other directly in the same process, one release ships all changes, and a stack trace can show the whole path."],"callout":"Many large companies ran monoliths for years. A monolith is a good architecture until it no longer fits the team or workload."},{"title":"Many teams share one codebase","body":["The company grows to 50 engineers across six teams. Each team’s changes go into one weekly release, so one bug can delay everyone. A memory leak in the new reports feature can crash the whole application, including checkout. And if search needs more memory, every feature must be scaled along with it."],"callout":"Monolith problems often come from team coordination: teams wait on each other, and one feature’s bug can affect the whole app."},{"title":"Split by business capability","body":["Break the app into <span class=\"term\">microservices</span> around business areas such as Users, Catalog, Orders, and Payments. Each service is a separate program, owned by a team and deployed on its own schedule. An <b>API gateway</b> gives client apps one front door and routes each request to the right service."],"callout":"Split around business capabilities, not technical layers. A “database service” or “UI service” may only move the dependency elsewhere."},{"title":"Give each service its own data","body":["If services still share one database, they remain tied together: one team’s schema change can break other teams. Give each service its own database and let only that service access it. If Orders needs payment data, it asks Payments through an API or listens for its events."],"callout":"A database per service allows independent deployments, but removes joins and transactions across services. See <em>Sagas</em>."},{"title":"Network calls can fail","body":["Showing one order page now takes six network hops: gateway to Orders, Orders to Payments, plus calls to Users and Catalog. Each network hop adds delay and can fail or become slow. Debugging can span four codebases. The system needs timeouts, retries, tracing, and dashboards to handle these failures."],"callout":"A function call inside one program either returns or raises an error. A network call can fail partway, arrive late, or get no response."},{"title":"Avoid a distributed monolith","body":["One company split its app into 12 services but kept services reading one another’s tables. Each feature still needed changes in five services, all released together. The company got the network overhead of microservices and the close dependencies of a monolith. Releases became slower."],"callout":"If services must always be deployed together, they still behave like one service. Clear boundaries should come before the split."},{"title":"Order history needs catalog data","body":["The order-history page shows each order with product names and photos. In one database, a SQL join could combine this data. Now orders are in Orders and products are in Catalog, so there is no join across the services. Orders calls Catalog once for each order: 20 orders mean 20 calls. This is the <b>N+1 problem</b>. The page slows down, and it also depends on Catalog being available."],"callout":"Screens that combine data from many services can be difficult. Plan for them when choosing service boundaries."},{"title":"Keep a local copy for reading","body":["Orders can keep copies of the catalog fields it displays, such as product name and photo. Catalog publishes a <code>ProductUpdated</code> event when they change, and Orders updates its local copy. Order history can use one query in Orders’ database and still work if Catalog is down. The price on an old order remains the price at purchase, because that is historical data."],"callout":"The copy is <span class=\"term\">eventually consistent</span>: a changed product name may take a moment to appear. That delay is acceptable for order history."},{"title":"Test API contracts between teams","body":["The Payments team renames <code>amount</code> to <code>amount_cents</code>. Its own tests pass, but Orders still expects the old field, so checkout fails after the release. <span class=\"term\">Consumer-driven contract tests</span> let Orders publish the fields it depends on. Payments’ build checks those contracts before release and blocks a breaking change before production."],"callout":"Combine contract tests with API versioning: add fields freely, and remove them only when no contract needs them."},{"title":"Choose one application or many","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Monolith</th><th>Microservices</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Team size</td><td>One to a few teams</td><td>Many teams</td></tr><tr><td>Deployments</td><td>Together</td><td>Independent</td></tr><tr><td>Calls</td><td>In-process and fast</td><td>Network calls that can fail</td></tr><tr><td>Data</td><td>One database, joins, transactions</td><td>Database per service; sagas</td></tr><tr><td>Operations</td><td>Simple</td><td>Needs good tools</td></tr></tbody></table>A common path is a <b>modular monolith</b>: one deployable app with clear internal boundaries. Split out a module only when a team needs to deploy or scale it independently."],"callout":"Microservices can solve organizational scaling problems. Without those problems, they often add cost and complexity."},{"title":"Migrate one capability at a time","body":["Do not rewrite the whole app at once. Put the gateway in front of the monolith, build one new Payments service, and route only <code>/payments/*</code> to it. Other requests still go to the monolith. Move one business capability at a time until the monolith can be retired. Each step can be shipped and reversed."],"callout":"This gradual migration is called the <b>strangler fig</b> pattern, named after a plant that grows around a tree until it can stand on its own."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>A <b>monolith</b> is simple and fast until many teams need to work independently.</li><li><b>Microservices</b> split by business capability, and each service owns its data.</li><li>They add network calls, partial failures, and operational work.</li><li>Use <b>local read copies</b> instead of cross-service joins; protect changes with <b>contract tests</b>.</li><li>Avoid a <b>distributed monolith</b>. Migrate gradually with the <b>strangler fig</b> pattern.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Service Discovery</b> — how services find each other as instances come and go."}]},"service-discovery":{"summary":"Help services find current instances and give outside clients one stable entry point.","goals":["Why fixed server addresses become outdated","How registries, heartbeats, and DNS keep addresses current","How client-side and server-side discovery differ","How readiness checks, slow start, and draining work","How a registry keeps working through node failures","How service meshes and API gateways help"],"steps":[{"title":"Orders needs to find Payments","body":["Orders needs to call Payments. One simple method is to put the payment servers’ IP addresses in Orders’ configuration and spread requests across them. This works only while those machines and addresses stay the same."],"callout":"In a monolith, calling Payments is just a function call in the same program. Separate services need to find one another over a network."},{"title":"Instances change over time","body":["Cloud servers are often replaced. An autoscaler adds or removes them, a deployment replaces them, and a failed server is swapped out. Payments moved from IP address 10.0.3.7 to 10.0.5.21, but Orders still has the old list. Half its calls fail, while the new instance gets no traffic."],"callout":"An address written into configuration can become outdated. In an autoscaled system this can happen many times a day."},{"title":"Ask a service registry","body":["Each running instance <b>registers</b> with a <span class=\"term\">service registry</span> and sends regular <b>heartbeats</b> to show it is alive. Orders asks the registry for the current Payments addresses. Consul, etcd, ZooKeeper, and Eureka are examples. Kubernetes also provides service discovery for its services."],"callout":"Orders can cache the address list for a few seconds instead of asking on every request, so the registry does not become a bottleneck."},{"title":"Instances register and leave automatically","body":["A new instance at 10.0.6.4 starts, registers itself, and can receive traffic within seconds. Another instance hangs and misses three heartbeats, so the registry removes it. No one has to edit a configuration file."],"callout":"A heartbeat gives the registry information it can use to decide whether an instance is responding. Load balancers use a similar health check."},{"title":"Let the client or a server choose","body":["With <b>client-side discovery</b>, Orders gets the address list and chooses an instance, perhaps using round robin or load. There is no extra network hop, but every client needs this logic. With <b>server-side discovery</b>, Orders calls one stable name. An internal load balancer or Kubernetes Service finds an instance and forwards the request. Clients stay simpler."],"callout":"Kubernetes gives each service a stable name and virtual IP, so many teams do not work with the registry directly."},{"title":"Use DNS for discovery","body":["A registry can answer through <span class=\"term\">DNS</span>. Looking up <code>payments.internal</code> returns IP addresses for healthy instances. Most languages and tools already support DNS. The risk is caching: a client may keep an old answer for its full <span class=\"term\">TTL</span> (time to live) or even longer, and keep calling an instance that is gone. Use short TTLs and retry connection errors."],"callout":"Some runtimes keep DNS answers for a very long time. Check their behavior before relying on DNS for service discovery."},{"title":"Do not send traffic before an instance is ready","body":["A new instance registers as soon as its process starts, before it has connected to the database or warmed its cache. The registry sends it a full share of traffic, and a third of payment calls time out for 30 seconds. Being <em>alive</em> does not mean it is <em>ready</em> to serve requests."],"callout":"Cold starts often happen during deployments and autoscaling, when traffic is already moving between instances."},{"title":"Check readiness, ramp up, and drain","body":["Use separate checks for <b>liveness</b> (“is the process running?”) and <b>readiness</b> (“can it serve requests now?”). Add an instance only after it passes its readiness check. Increase its traffic share gradually; this is <span class=\"term\">slow start</span>. Before stopping an instance, remove it from discovery, let requests already in progress finish (<b>draining</b>), then shut it down."],"callout":"Kubernetes includes readinessProbe, livenessProbe, and a preStop hook with a grace period for draining."},{"title":"Keep the registry available too","body":["Callers cache the last known address list. If the registry is unreachable, they can continue using that list for a while, though they will not see changes. The registry itself runs as a small <b>cluster</b> of three or five nodes that agree by majority vote using an algorithm such as Raft or Paxos. A three-node cluster can lose one node; a five-node cluster can lose two."],"callout":"etcd, Consul, and ZooKeeper use consensus clusters. Odd sizes are useful: three nodes survive one failure, and five survive two."},{"title":"Do not rebuild network features in every service","body":["Discovery is only one part of service communication. Each call may also need timeouts, retries, circuit breakers, encryption, and metrics. If teams use different programming languages, each may build its own version, with its own bugs and defaults."],"callout":"Several different retry implementations can behave differently during the same outage."},{"title":"Use a service mesh for shared plumbing","body":["A <span class=\"term\">service mesh</span>, such as Istio or Linkerd, runs a small proxy called a <b>sidecar</b> beside each service. A service talks to its local proxy. The proxies find instances, retry, time out, encrypt traffic with mTLS, and report metrics. A <b>control plane</b> sends current addresses and rules to the proxies."],"callout":"A mesh adds two local network hops and operational complexity. It can help many services, but may be too much for a small system."},{"title":"Give outside clients one front door","body":["Service discovery handles calls between services. Outside clients, such as apps and browsers, can use an <span class=\"term\">API gateway</span> as one front door. It authenticates requests, applies rate limits, handles TLS, and routes each path to the right service. Clients do not need to know the internal service layout."],"callout":"Different clients may use a <b>backend for frontend (BFF)</b>: a gateway shaped for the needs of mobile or web clients."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Fixed addresses become outdated as service instances change.</li><li>A <b>registry</b> uses heartbeats to keep an address list current; DNS can provide another route.</li><li>Send traffic only to <b>ready</b> instances; drain requests before stopping one.</li><li>Discovery can happen in a client, proxy, or <b>service mesh</b>.</li><li>An <b>API gateway</b> gives outside clients one stable front door.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Sagas</b> — keep data consistent when one action uses several services."}]},"sagas":{"summary":"Coordinate work across services with separate databases, and handle failure without one long transaction.","goals":["Why a normal transaction cannot cover several services","How two-phase commit works and why it can block","How sagas use compensating actions","How orchestration and choreography differ","How to handle failed undos and unknown outcomes"],"steps":[{"title":"One trip uses three databases","body":["A traveler books a flight, hotel, and rental car. Each belongs to a different service with its own database. The flight booking succeeds, then the hotel booking succeeds, but the car service has no cars left. The traveler now has a flight and hotel they cannot use, and may have been charged for both."],"callout":"A database transaction works inside one database. Across services, there is no built-in command to undo every change."},{"title":"Try two-phase commit","body":["One option is <span class=\"term\">two-phase commit</span> (2PC). First, a coordinator asks every service to <b>prepare</b>: do the work, hold the needed rows, and promise it can finish. If all services say yes, the coordinator tells them to <b>commit</b>. If one says no, they all roll back. Here, the car service says no, so nothing is booked."],"callout":"Some databases and XA transactions use 2PC. Every participant must support it, but many web APIs and NoSQL stores do not."},{"title":"The coordinator can leave work stuck","body":["After a service prepares, it must wait for the coordinator’s decision. If the coordinator crashes before sending it, the flight and hotel services hold locks on seats and rooms. They cannot commit or roll back on their own. Other travelers must wait until the coordinator recovers. The trip remains all-or-nothing, but it cannot finish."],"callout":"2PC can block: one slow or failed coordinator can leave every participant waiting. This is often a poor fit across services and networks."},{"title":"Use a saga to undo completed steps","body":["A <span class=\"term\">saga</span> splits the trip into short transactions, each committed in its own service: book a flight, then a hotel, then a car. Nothing stays locked across the whole trip. Each step has a <b>compensating action</b> that counteracts its effect. If the car booking fails, cancel the hotel, then cancel the flight."],"callout":"A saga avoids long locks but gives up isolation: other users may briefly see some steps completed while later steps are still running."},{"title":"Orchestrate the steps","body":["In an <b>orchestrated</b> saga, one component called the orchestrator tells each service what to do, waits for its reply, and chooses whether to continue or compensate. It saves every step in its own database. If it crashes, it can resume from the last saved state."],"callout":"Workflow tools such as Temporal, AWS Step Functions, and Camunda are built to save, resume, and monitor workflows."},{"title":"Let services react to events","body":["In a <b>choreographed</b> saga, there is no central coordinator. Flights publishes <code>FlightBooked</code>. Hotels listens, books a room, and publishes <code>HotelBooked</code>. Cars listens and rents a car—or publishes <code>CarFailed</code>, which tells Hotels and Flights to cancel. The flow is spread across services, so it can be harder to follow and change."],"callout":"Choreography can suit a short workflow with few steps. A growing saga is often easier to reason about with an orchestrator."},{"title":"A saga has no isolation","body":["While a saga runs, other people may see a partial result: the flight is reserved but the hotel is not yet booked. Some actions cannot truly be undone. An email confirming a flight has already been sent. Design around this: use a <b>pending</b> or <b>reserved</b> state, do likely-to-fail steps first, and do hard-to-undo steps—such as charging a card or sending an email—last."],"callout":"A compensation is a new action, not a rollback. Refunding a charge is not the same as never charging the card."},{"title":"Resume safely after a crash","body":["The orchestrator crashes after Hotels books a room but before it saves that fact. On restart it sees “hotel: pending” and sends the booking request again. Without protection, the traveler could get two rooms. Send an <b>idempotency key</b>, such as the saga ID plus the step, so Hotels recognizes the duplicate and returns the existing booking."],"callout":"Sagas may retry a step, so each step and each compensation must be safe to run more than once."},{"title":"Retry a compensation that fails","body":["The car booking fails, so the saga cancels the hotel. But the hotel API is down. The saga should keep retrying the cancellation with backoff. The cancellation must be safe to repeat. If it is still stuck after a few hours, put it in a queue for a person to resolve."],"callout":"A compensation should be able to succeed eventually. Alert when one is stuck."},{"title":"A timeout leaves the result unknown","body":["The car booking request times out. The car service may have booked it before the connection failed, or it may not have. A timeout means the result is <b>unknown</b>, not necessarily failed. If the saga compensates blindly, it might cancel a booking that never existed—or leave one that did. Query the car service for request <code>81-car</code>, or retry with the same idempotency key to learn the real result."],"callout":"Treat a remote step as succeeded, failed, or unknown. Only a known result lets the saga choose its next action."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>A normal database transaction cannot cover several independent services.</li><li><b>Two-phase commit</b> is all-or-nothing, but can block if its coordinator fails.</li><li>A <b>saga</b> commits steps separately and runs <b>compensations</b> after failure.</li><li>Choose an orchestrator or event choreography; make every step idempotent.</li><li>Retry compensations, and treat timeouts as <b>unknown</b> until checked.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Batch &amp; Stream Processing</b> — process huge volumes of events overnight or as they arrive."}]},"batch-stream":{"summary":"Analyze huge amounts of event data in large batches or continuously as it arrives.","goals":["Keep large analytics scans off the production database","How batch jobs split work with MapReduce","How to handle uneven or very popular keys","How stream processing and time windows work","How to handle late events, lag, and partitions","How approximate counts and batch-stream combinations work"],"steps":[{"title":"Analytics can slow the live app","body":["Every video view creates a row in the production database—about two billion rows each day. A dashboard recalculates views per video every minute with a large <code>GROUP BY</code> query. Each run scans more of the day’s data. By mid-morning, it may read hundreds of millions of rows and compete with the database work that serves the app."],"callout":"Production databases are tuned for many small, quick queries. Large scans use their memory, CPU, and disk."},{"title":"Batch: collect first, calculate later","body":["Instead, save each view as an event in inexpensive <b>object storage</b>. Once per night, a <span class=\"term\">batch job</span> reads the day’s files, calculates counts using several worker machines, and writes the results to an analytics <b>warehouse</b>. The dashboard reads from the warehouse. The production database can focus on the app again."],"callout":"Batch jobs are simple and easy to rerun. If a job has a bug, fix it and process the same files again."},{"title":"Split a batch job into three parts","body":["Two billion events take too long for one machine, so split the work. <b>Map</b>: each worker reads a portion of the files and counts views by video. <b>Shuffle</b>: send counts for the same video to one worker. <b>Reduce</b>: that worker adds the counts. Adding workers can make the job finish faster. This pattern is called <span class=\"term\">MapReduce</span>."],"callout":"Spark, BigQuery, and Snowflake use this kind of parallel work, often behind a SQL interface."},{"title":"One popular video can slow the job","body":["One video went viral and got 40% of yesterday’s views. During the shuffle, all of those views go to the same reducer. That worker spends three hours on the video while the other 99 finish in five minutes. The whole job waits for the slowest worker. Splitting by key only balances work when the keys have similar amounts of data."],"callout":"Uneven key sizes are called <b>skew</b>. Look for one task that takes much longer than the rest."},{"title":"Combine counts before sending them","body":["A <span class=\"term\">combiner</span> adds counts on each mapper first. Instead of sending 800 million separate “view of 4f2” rows, each mapper sends one partial count. For a key that is still too popular, use <span class=\"term\">salting</span>: split it into keys such as <code>4f2#0</code> through <code>4f2#9</code> and send them to ten reducers. A final step adds the ten totals. Here the job drops from three hours to about 20 minutes."],"callout":"Spark and many SQL engines combine sums and counts automatically. They can also detect and split skewed keys in joins."},{"title":"Some results cannot wait until tomorrow","body":["A video going viral should appear in “Trending” now, not tomorrow. A bot farm faking views should be caught within minutes. Running the batch job every hour helps, but each run still rereads a large amount of data."],"callout":"Batch processing answers “what happened?” Some products also need to know “what is happening?”"},{"title":"Process events as they arrive","body":["The app publishes each view to an <b>event stream</b>, such as Kafka. A <span class=\"term\">stream processor</span>, such as Flink, Kafka Streams, or Spark Streaming, reads events as they arrive, keeps running counts, and writes results to a fast store. The dashboard can now be seconds behind instead of a day."],"callout":"The processor keeps its current counts as local state and saves checkpoints. After a crash, it can resume from the latest checkpoint instead of starting over."},{"title":"Count events in time windows","body":["A stream does not end, so “count views” needs a time range called a <span class=\"term\">window</span>. <b>Tumbling windows</b> divide time into fixed blocks, such as views per minute. <b>Sliding windows</b> overlap, such as the last 10 minutes updated every minute. A sudden increase between windows may show a trend—or fraud."],"callout":"<b>Session windows</b> group events by bursts of activity. They can help answer questions such as “how long did people watch?”"},{"title":"Handle events that arrive late or twice","body":["A phone watches videos in the subway without a signal, then uploads its views two hours later. Should each view count at 12:04, when it happened, or now, when it arrived? A flaky connection may also send the same event twice. Stream processors use <b>event time</b> (when it happened), a <span class=\"term\">watermark</span> (a signal for when a time window is complete enough to publish), a rule for late events, and event IDs to drop duplicates."],"callout":"“Exactly once” usually means checkpoints and safe, repeatable writes prevent duplicate effects. This is the same challenge as queue processing."},{"title":"A processor can fall behind","body":["A celebrity’s live stream triples traffic for an hour. The processor cannot keep up, so unprocessed events accumulate. This is <span class=\"term\">consumer lag</span>. Here lag reaches four minutes and 18 million events. The stream still has the events, but the “live” dashboard is four minutes old and fraud checks run late."],"callout":"Consumer lag is a key health measure for a stream. Alert on how many events or seconds behind the processor is."},{"title":"Add processors across partitions","body":["Split the stream into <b>partitions</b>, for example by video ID, so events for one video stay in order. Each processor instance handles some partitions. Adding another processor increases parallel work. In this example, two processors handle two partitions each; the backlog drains and lag falls to seconds. An autoscaler can add processors when lag grows."],"callout":"The number of partitions limits parallel processing. Create enough partitions for future growth; adding them later can move keys between partitions."},{"title":"Estimate unique viewers","body":["Counting all views is simple. Counting <b>unique</b> viewers exactly means remembering every viewer ID. One popular video may have 50 million IDs—about 800 MB of memory for one video in one time window. A <span class=\"term\">HyperLogLog</span> sketch estimates the number of distinct IDs using about 12 KB, with around 1% error. Sketches from different machines or windows can be merged."],"callout":"Approximate algorithms such as HyperLogLog, count-min sketch, and t-digest trade a small amount of precision for large savings. Dashboards rarely need perfect counts."},{"title":"Run batch and stream together","body":["Some systems use both: a stream gives fast, approximate answers, and a nightly batch recomputes exact counts from the full log. This is the <b>lambda architecture</b>. Other systems use only the stream and replay the saved event log through a new processor to rebuild results. This is the <b>kappa architecture</b>. <table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Batch</th><th>Stream</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Freshness</td><td>Hours</td><td>Seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Complexity</td><td>Low</td><td>Higher</td></tr><tr><td>Rerun</td><td>Easy</td><td>Replay the log</td></tr></tbody></table>"],"callout":"Start with batch unless you clearly need results within seconds. Add streaming for the results that do."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Keep heavy analytics scans <b>off the production database</b>.</li><li><b>Batch</b> jobs process finished data cheaply; results may be hours old.</li><li><b>Stream</b> processors handle events as they arrive; results can be seconds old, with more parts to manage.</li><li>Watch for <b>skew</b> and <b>consumer lag</b>; use combiners, salting, partitions, and processors as needed.</li><li>Plan for <b>late</b> and <b>duplicate</b> events. Many systems use batch and stream processing together.</li></ul>"],"callout":"That completes Architecture. Next: <b>Security</b> — starting with authentication."}]},"authentication":{"summary":"Verify who a person is while protecting passwords, sessions, and sign-in tokens.","goals":["Store passwords as salted, deliberately slow hashes","How sessions and password resets work","How to reduce credential-stuffing attacks","Why passkeys resist phishing","How identity providers and short-lived tokens work"],"steps":[{"title":"A database full of passwords","body":["Alice signs up, and the login service saves her password exactly as she typed it. Logging in is then a simple comparison. Later, a database backup leaks because a storage bucket was misconfigured or a laptop was stolen. The passwords are immediately readable. People often reuse passwords, so an attacker may try them on email, banking, and other sites."],"callout":"Plan for the possibility that a database may leak. Ask what an attacker could do with the data."},{"title":"Store a slow, salted hash","body":["Do not store the password itself. Store a <span class=\"term\">hash</span>, a one-way value calculated from it. At login, calculate a hash from the entered password and compare it with the saved value. Use a password-hashing method such as <b>Argon2id</b>, <b>scrypt</b>, or <b>bcrypt</b>. A random <b>salt</b> for each user makes identical passwords produce different stored values. These methods are deliberately costly to slow down guesses if the database leaks."],"callout":"Fast general-purpose hashes such as MD5 or SHA-256 are not suited to password storage: specialized hardware can try many guesses quickly. Tune a password-hashing method for the service’s expected load."},{"title":"Create a session after login","body":["People should not need to type a password on every page. After a successful login, the service creates a <b>session</b> and gives the browser a random session ID in a cookie. Later requests send that cookie. Set <code>HttpOnly</code> so page scripts cannot read it, <code>Secure</code> so it is sent only over HTTPS, and <code>SameSite</code> to control when it is sent with cross-site requests. Another option is a short-lived signed token; see <em>Stateless Services</em>."],"callout":"Treat a session ID like a password: make it long and random, keep it out of URLs and logs, and invalidate it at logout."},{"title":"Make password reset safe","body":["Password reset is another way into an account and can be weaker than login. Reset links should not use guessable or long-lived tokens. A safe flow uses a long random token once, expires it after 15 minutes, and gives the same reply whether or not the email belongs to an account. After a password change, sign out the user’s existing sessions."],"callout":"Anything that can change a password—reset, email change, or support tools—needs protection like the login flow."},{"title":"Stop credential stuffing","body":["A bot has a list of a billion email and password pairs leaked from other websites. It tries them against your login form, thousands per second. Password hashing can protect a leaked database, but it cannot stop people from reusing passwords. In this example, about 0.5% of attempts succeed—enough to take over thousands of accounts. This is <span class=\"term\">credential stuffing</span>."],"callout":"Credential stuffing uses passwords leaked elsewhere. It does not require a bug in your own code."},{"title":"Make automated guessing harder","body":["Limit failed attempts per account and IP address. Add a challenge when traffic looks automated. At signup, reject passwords found in known breach lists. Flag risky sign-ins, such as a new device or country, and ask for another way to prove the user’s identity."],"callout":"Services such as Have I Been Pwned can check whether a password appears in known breaches without sending the full password."},{"title":"Require another factor","body":["<span class=\"term\">Multi-factor authentication</span> asks for proof from more than one category: something the person knows, such as a password, and something they have, such as an authenticator app, phone approval, or hardware key. A stolen password alone is not enough. <b>Passkeys</b> use a device-held key pair and can resist phishing."],"callout":"SMS codes are better than no second factor, but can be intercepted through SIM swapping. Prefer an authenticator app, device approval, or passkey when possible."},{"title":"A fake login page can relay codes","body":["An email warns about an “unusual sign-in” and links to <code>shop-example.co</code>, a copy of your login page. Alice enters her password and the one-time code from her app. The fake site forwards both to the real site immediately and steals her session. A code or approval can stop someone using a stolen password later, but may not stop a live phishing site relaying it."],"callout":"A fake page can ask for anything a person can read and type. Training helps, but cannot prevent every phishing attack."},{"title":"Use a passkey instead of typing","body":["A <span class=\"term\">passkey</span>, built on WebAuthn, uses a public and private key pair on Alice’s phone or laptop. The site sends a challenge, and the device signs it. The signature is tied to the real site domain, <code>shop.example</code>. On the fake site, the device will not offer that passkey. There is no password or one-time code to relay. The server stores only the public key, which cannot be used to sign in as Alice."],"callout":"Passkeys may sync across a person’s devices through Apple, Google, or a password manager, so losing one phone does not always mean losing access."},{"title":"Let an identity provider sign users in","body":["“Sign in with Google” uses <span class=\"term\">OpenID Connect</span>, an identity layer built on OAuth 2.0. Your app sends Alice to an <b>identity provider</b>. She signs in there, and it returns a signed <b>ID token</b> that says who she is. Your service validates the token, including its signature, issuer, intended audience, and expiry. It does not need to see or store her password. Companies use this approach for single sign-on with services such as Okta or Entra ID."],"callout":"Authentication is difficult to implement safely. Using a specialist identity provider can be a good choice."},{"title":"Limit the damage from a stolen token","body":["A bug logs request headers, and an attacker finds Alice’s token. While valid, a token can let the attacker act as Alice. If it never expires, the access could last indefinitely. Use short-lived <b>access tokens</b>, such as 15 minutes, plus a <b>refresh token</b> stored carefully and able to be <b>revoked</b>. Logging out—or choosing “log out everywhere”—can revoke it."],"callout":"Short expiry limits how long a leaked token is useful. Revocation lets the service end access sooner."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Store passwords as <b>slow, salted hashes</b>, not readable text.</li><li>Protect session cookies with <b>HttpOnly</b>, <b>Secure</b>, and an appropriate <b>SameSite</b> setting.</li><li>Reduce credential stuffing with rate limits, breach checks, and another factor.</li><li>Make password reset as safe as login; <b>passkeys</b> can resist phishing.</li><li>Consider an identity provider and keep tokens short-lived and revocable.</li></ul>"],"callout":"Next: <b>Authorization</b> — knowing who someone is does not say what they are allowed to do."}]},"authorization":{"summary":"Authentication identifies a person. Authorization decides what that person may see or do.","goals":["How broken object-level authorization exposes another user’s data","How ownership checks and field allow-lists protect requests","How database row-level security adds a second check","How roles, relationships, and central policies work","How least privilege prevents a confused deputy","How audit logs record sensitive access"],"steps":[{"callout":"Hiding a button in the app is not authorization. A person can call the API directly and send any ID."},{"body":["For every request, check whether the requested invoice belongs to the caller’s organization. If not, return no invoice. Deny access by default: an endpoint with a missing check should fail closed."],"callout":"A 404 response can avoid confirming that another person’s object exists. Random IDs may make guessing harder, but they do not replace an access check."},{"callout":"Frameworks that copy request fields directly into database models make this easy to get wrong. List the fields each endpoint may change."},{"callout":"This is defense in depth: assume one check may be missed someday, and add another layer."},{"callout":"Keep roles few and clear. Too many special-case roles become difficult to understand and review."},{"body":["Roles do not express every sharing rule, such as “Alice can see this folder because it was shared with her team.” <b>Relationship-based access</b> (ReBAC) stores facts about folders, teams, and people, then follows those relationships to decide access. This supports sharing like Google Docs."]},{"body":["If services each have their own permission checks, the rules can drift. Each service can ask a <span class=\"term\">policy service</span>, “May Alice edit invoice 1042?” The application still enforces the answer. The rules live in one place, can be tested, and decisions can be logged."]},{"callout":"A component’s permissions determine how much damage its bugs can cause."},{"body":["Give each service and person only the access needed for their job. The reports service gets a read-only account that can see invoices and nothing else. Give each service its own identity and credentials. A bug in Reports can then expose only the data it already needs."]},{"body":["Alice asks for a report of invoices over $10,000. The reports service queries with its own powerful identity and can read every company’s invoices. It has been tricked into using its authority for Alice. This is a <span class=\"term\">confused deputy</span>."]},{"body":["Pass Alice’s identity to the reports service with her token, or exchange it for a narrower token. The query then uses Alice’s organization and permissions. Use the service’s own powerful credentials only for work no user requested, such as nightly maintenance."]},{"body":["Record sensitive access in an append-only <span class=\"term\">audit log</span>: who accessed what, when, and from where. The people being audited should not be able to edit the log. It can help investigate an incident and can trigger alerts for unusual activity, such as opening 5,000 invoices in ten minutes."],"callout":"Audit-log requirements depend on the laws, contracts, and standards that apply to the service."},{"body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Check whether the caller can access <b>this object</b> on every request.</li><li>Use <b>roles</b> for kinds of users and <b>relationships</b> for sharing.</li><li>Define policies centrally and enforce them in each service.</li><li>Allow-list writable fields and add database <b>row-level security</b>.</li><li>Grant <b>least privilege</b>, act with the user’s permissions, and keep an audit log.</li></ul>"]}]},"encryption":{"summary":"Protect data in transit and at rest, and keep the keys and credentials safe.","goals":["How TLS and certificates protect network traffic","Why to encrypt inside a data center and on disk","How key rotation works","How to keep secrets out of source code","When tokenization or end-to-end encryption helps"],"steps":[{"callout":"Anyone along the network path can read or alter traffic that is not encrypted."},{"body":["With HTTPS, <span class=\"term\">TLS</span> encrypts data between Alice’s browser and the site. During setup, the server presents a <b>certificate</b> signed by a trusted certificate authority. The browser uses it to check that the server belongs to <code>shop.example</code>. A person copying the network packets sees encrypted data and cannot quietly change it."]},{"callout":"Short-lived certificates encourage automation and limit how long a leaked key can be used."},{"body":["The load balancer <b>terminates</b> TLS: it decrypts the browser’s traffic. Some systems then sent plain traffic to internal services. With <b>mutual TLS</b> (mTLS), both services present certificates. Each network hop is encrypted, and both sides check the other’s identity."]},{"callout":"Encryption in transit protects network traffic. A copied backup also needs protection."},{"body":["Encrypt database files, disks, and backups. Keep the keys separate from the data. A <span class=\"term\">key management service</span> (KMS) controls a master key. With <b>envelope encryption</b>, the app encrypts data with a data key, then encrypts that data key with the KMS master key. KMS access can be restricted and logged."]},{"body":["Change keys on a schedule or if one may have leaked. Re-encrypting terabytes can take days. With envelope encryption, create a new master key and <b>re-wrap</b> each small data key with it. The large data itself does not need to be encrypted again."]},{"body":["A developer puts the payment API key in source code, and the repository is public. Automated scanners can find it quickly. Deleting the line does not remove it from the repository history. Revoke the exposed key and issue a replacement."]},{"body":["Keep credentials out of code. The app proves its identity to a <span class=\"term\">secrets manager</span> and retrieves only the secrets it needs. Rotate secrets or issue short-lived ones. CI scanners can block commits that look like credentials."]},{"body":["Handling card data can bring systems into the scope of <b>PCI DSS</b> requirements. Instead of storing the card number, send it to a payment provider. The provider stores it and returns a <span class=\"term\">token</span>, such as <code>tok_4f2c</code>. Your database, logs, and analytics store only the token."]},{"body":["With <span class=\"term\">end-to-end encryption</span>, Alice’s device encrypts a message so only Bob’s device can decrypt it. The chat server stores and forwards encrypted text. A server breach does not reveal message contents. Real protocols use keys to agree on fresh secret keys; <b>forward secrecy</b> means a later stolen key cannot unlock older messages."]},{"body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li>Use <b>TLS</b> for outside connections and <b>mTLS</b> between services when needed.</li><li>Encrypt stored data and keep keys in a separate <b>KMS</b>.</li><li>Automate certificate renewal; rotate keys by re-wrapping data keys.</li><li>Keep credentials in a <b>secrets manager</b>, rotate them, and scan for leaks.</li><li>Use <b>tokens</b> instead of storing sensitive values such as card numbers.</li><li>Use <b>end-to-end encryption</b> when the server should not read messages.</li></ul>"]}]},"testing":{"summary":"Use fast tests for individual logic and a smaller number of slower tests for the full system.","goals":["How unit tests and test doubles work","How integration and contract tests catch different problems","How end-to-end tests fit into the test pyramid","How to handle flaky tests and test load and failure"],"steps":[{"callout":"Every change raises the question: what else could it break? Tests let the team check repeatedly instead of relying on memory."},{"callout":"The later a bug is found, the more it costs: seconds in a test, hours in review, or days in production."},{"body":["A <span class=\"term\">unit test</span> calls one function with known inputs and checks its result. It runs in milliseconds without a database or network, so teams can write many and run them on every change. A rounding example becomes a test that fails if the bug returns."]},{"body":["Checkout depends on other systems, so unit tests can use <span class=\"term\">test doubles</span>. A <b>fake</b> database works in memory. A <b>stub</b> always returns a planned answer. A <b>mock</b> records whether something happened, such as charging a card once. A double may not behave like the real service, so tests need to cover real integrations too."]},{"body":["An <span class=\"term\">integration test</span> runs Checkout against a real database, often started in a container for the test. It checks what was actually saved and can catch SQL mistakes, broken migrations, or mismatched types. Keep the payment provider stubbed so no real card is charged; a smaller suite can use its sandbox."]},{"body":["A <span class=\"term\">contract test</span> records what Checkout expects from Inventory, such as a request and a response with certain fields. Inventory checks its changes against that contract, so a breaking API change fails before deployment."]},{"callout":"An end-to-end test shows that something broke, but not why. Faster tests below it should help locate the cause."},{"body":["The <span class=\"term\">test pyramid</span> has many fast unit tests at the bottom, fewer integration and contract tests in the middle, and a handful of end-to-end tests at the top. Put each check in the lowest layer that can verify it."]},{"body":["A <span class=\"term\">flaky test</span> sometimes fails even when the code has not changed. This test waits exactly two seconds for an email, but the email sometimes takes three. People learn to rerun it and may rerun real failures too."]},{"body":["A <b>load test</b> sends production-like traffic to a copy of the system and finds when latency starts to rise. <b>Failure tests</b> deliberately stop a database replica or slow the payment provider, then check whether timeouts, retries, and failover work."]},{"body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li><b>Unit tests</b> check one part with test doubles and run quickly.</li><li><b>Integration tests</b> use real neighboring systems, such as a database in a container.</li><li><b>Contract tests</b> catch breaking API changes between teams.</li><li>A few <b>end-to-end tests</b> cover important journeys; keep the pyramid right-side up.</li><li>Fix <b>flaky</b> tests and test for load and failure, not just correct output.</li></ul>"]}]},"cicd":{"summary":"Build, test, and release changes in small steps, with a quick way to roll back.","goals":["How continuous integration checks each change","How one tested artifact moves between environments","How blue-green and canary releases work","How to change a database while old code still runs","How feature flags separate deployment from release"],"steps":[{"callout":"Large, infrequent releases can feel safer, but many changes at once are harder to test, diagnose, and undo."},{"callout":"Builds that depend on one person’s computer and deployments that cannot be repeated exactly make every release an experiment."},{"body":["Every push to the shared repository starts a <span class=\"term\">pipeline</span> on a clean machine. It builds the code and runs tests. The team merges small changes into <code>main</code> often and fixes a failed pipeline before adding more work. Keeping <code>main</code> buildable and tested is <span class=\"term\">continuous integration</span>."]},{"body":["The pipeline packages the result as an <b>immutable artifact</b>, often a container image tagged with its source commit. It stores the image in a registry. Test and deploy that same image in staging and production; only configuration changes."]},{"body":["Passing images deploy to <b>staging</b>, a small copy of production, where smoke tests check main user flows. With <span class=\"term\">continuous delivery</span>, a person can release the tested change. With continuous <b>deployment</b>, every passing change goes to production automatically."]},{"body":["With <span class=\"term\">blue-green deployment</span>, blue serves the current version while green starts the new version and warms up. A load balancer switches traffic to green. If something goes wrong, switch back to blue, which is still running."]},{"body":["A <span class=\"term\">canary</span> sends a small share of real traffic to the new version. Compare its errors and latency with the old version. If it is worse, roll it back while few users are affected. If healthy, increase its share gradually."]},{"body":["Version 42 renames database column <code>name</code> to <code>full_name</code>. Old servers still expect <code>name</code>, so they fail during rollout. Use <span class=\"term\">expand and contract</span>: add the new column and write both; copy existing values; switch reads; remove the old column only when no running code uses it. Each step works with old and new versions."]},{"body":["A <span class=\"term\">feature flag</span> controls who sees a feature, separately from deploying its code. Turn it on for the team, then 1% of users, then everyone. If it causes problems, switch it off without a new deployment. Remove old flags after release so they do not leave unused branches behind."]},{"body":["The <span class=\"term\">DORA metrics</span> measure how often a team deploys, how long changes take to reach production, how many deployments cause problems, and how quickly the team restores service. These measures help teams discuss both delivery speed and stability."]},{"body":["<ul class=\"takeaways\"><li><b>Continuous integration</b>: merge small changes often and build and test every push on a clean machine.</li><li><b>Build once</b>: promote the same immutable artifact through every environment.</li><li><b>Continuous delivery</b> keeps passing changes ready for production; continuous deployment releases them automatically.</li><li>Use <b>blue-green</b> to switch back, <b>canaries</b> to limit exposure, and <b>feature flags</b> to separate deploy from release.</li><li>Keep database changes compatible with old and new code: expand, migrate, then contract.</li></ul>"]}]},"hld-lld":{"summary":"Look at a system from two distances: how its services fit together and how one service works inside.","goals":["What high-level design decides","What low-level design decides","How contracts connect the two levels","How to move through a design session"],"steps":[{"title":"One system, two levels of detail","body":["Consider a food-delivery app. From far away, it has a few services—Orders, Couriers, and Payments—with their own data. They communicate through an API gateway and events. Up close, each box is a program with classes, database tables, and rules. These are <span class=\"term\">high-level design</span> (HLD) and <span class=\"term\">low-level design</span> (LLD)."],"callout":"Neither level is more real. They answer different questions for different readers and change at different speeds."},{"title":"High-level design is the map","body":["HLD decides what the main parts are and how they connect: which services exist, what data each owns, how they communicate, and what happens when traffic grows or a component fails. The course so far has mostly worked at this level. Its main output is a diagram with the reasons for each choice."],"callout":"A useful HLD can fit on one page. Detailed explanations of one box belong in that component’s LLD."},{"title":"Explain the trade-offs in the map","body":["Placing an order should not wait for a courier to be found, so Orders publishes an event for Couriers to process later. Payments needs transactions, so it uses a relational database. Courier locations change every few seconds and create tens of thousands of writes each second, so they use a fast key–value store that can overwrite old locations. Each choice follows from the data and workload."],"callout":"Every connection and data store on an HLD diagram is a choice with alternatives. Be ready to explain what the choice gives up."},{"title":"Low-level design opens one box","body":["Now zoom into the Orders service. LLD decides how that component is built: its modules or classes, the interfaces between them, its database tables and indexes, its algorithms, and how it handles errors. A controller accepts HTTP requests, an order service applies business rules, a repository talks to the database, and a client calls Payments."],"callout":"Small interfaces around databases and other services make the main logic easier to test. See <em>Testing</em>."},{"title":"Define states, data, and edge cases","body":["An order moves through states. The design must say which changes are allowed: it can be cancelled while <code>placed</code> or <code>paid</code>, but not after the kitchen starts. LLD also defines table columns and types, plus an index on <code>(user_id, created_at)</code> for “my orders.” It decides what to do if the payment call times out or the same order arrives twice."],"callout":"Many production bugs come from an unhandled state change, a missing index, or a retry that charges twice."},{"title":"Contracts connect the two levels","body":["HLD defines what a service promises at its edge: its API and the events it publishes. LLD decides how the service keeps that promise. This lets teams change code inside the service as long as <code>POST /orders</code> and <code>OrderPlaced</code> keep the same meaning."],"callout":"Changing a contract affects other teams. Old and new versions may need to work side by side for a while. See API versioning."},{"title":"Design from the outside in","body":["In an interview, one candidate may spend 20 minutes on class diagrams and never mention that courier locations arrive 40,000 times a second. Another may draw clear service boxes but not explain how an order avoids a double charge. Work <b>top-down</b>: clarify requirements, estimate the load, outline the API and data, draw the HLD, then examine the few hardest parts in more detail."],"callout":"A design session often follows: requirements → estimates → API → data model → HLD → deep dives → failure cases. The next walkthroughs use this order."},{"title":"Recap","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>HLD</th><th>LLD</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>View</td><td>Whole system</td><td>One component</td></tr><tr><td>Parts</td><td>Services, stores, queues</td><td>Classes, tables, states</td></tr><tr><td>Decides</td><td>Connections and data locations</td><td>How each part works inside</td></tr><tr><td>Changes</td><td>Less often, with agreement</td><td>Often, within a team</td></tr></tbody></table>This course focuses on HLD and looks inside a component when a decision depends on it. LLD, including object-oriented design and design patterns, is a subject of its own."],"callout":"Next: <b>Design a URL Shortener</b> — a complete walkthrough from a brief to a finished design."}]},"design-url-shortener":{"summary":"Design a link shortener from its requirements through redirects, storage, analytics, and abuse protection.","goals":["Turn the brief into requirements and estimates","Create short codes without collisions","Speed up redirects with a cache and choose redirect behavior","Store billions of key-to-URL records","Keep analytics and abuse checks off the redirect path"],"steps":[{"title":"Start with the brief","body":["Design a service like bit.ly. A person pastes a long URL and gets a short one such as <code>sho.rt/x7Kp2Qa</code>. Opening that code sends the browser to the original URL. Before drawing boxes, decide what “works” means. It must create links and redirect. It should also expire links and count clicks. Redirects should be fast and rarely fail; creating a link can take a little longer."],"callout":"For each design, ask what it must do, how much work it handles, and which qualities matter most when you must make a trade-off."},{"title":"Estimate the size","body":["Suppose the service creates <b>100 million</b> links each month, about 40 writes per second. Each link is opened about 100 times, so redirects average 4,000 per second and peak near <b>12,000</b>. Over five years, that is 6 billion links. At about 500 bytes each, they use <b>3 TB</b>. The system is read-heavy: roughly 100 reads for each write. Its data is large but simple."],"callout":"The read-to-write ratio suggests using a cache. Six billion simple key-to-value records suggest splitting storage across machines."},{"title":"Begin with the simplest design","body":["Use two endpoints. <code>POST /links</code> saves a URL and returns a code. <code>GET /x7Kp2Qa</code> looks up the code and redirects. A table can hold <code>code</code> as the primary key, <code>long_url</code>, <code>created_at</code>, and <code>expires_at</code>. Stateless API servers behind a load balancer and one database are a good first version."],"callout":"Start with what meets the brief, then ask what will become the first limit. We still need a way to create codes and handle 12,000 reads per second."},{"title":"Why not use a shortened hash?","body":["One idea is to hash the long URL and use its first seven characters. This needs no counter, and the same URL always gets the same code. But seven characters cannot hold the whole hash, so different URLs will sometimes have the same code. With billions of links, collisions become common. Each insert must check whether the code is already taken and try again if it is. Two people shortening the same URL also get the same code, so they cannot have separate expiry times or click counts."],"callout":"There are about 3.5 trillion seven-character codes. The birthday effect means a first collision is likely after only about two million generated codes."},{"title":"Count IDs and encode them","body":["Give every link a unique number, then write it in <span class=\"term\">base 62</span>, which uses digits, lowercase letters, and uppercase letters. A unique number gives a unique code, so there is no collision check. Seven base-62 characters can represent about 62⁷, or 3.5 trillion, links. Use an ID block service: each API server reserves a block of one million IDs and counts through that block locally."],"callout":"Base-62 encoding repeatedly divides by 62 and records the remainder. For example, 125 becomes “21”: 2 × 62 + 1. Decoding reverses the steps."},{"title":"Make codes hard to guess","body":["Counter-based codes are sequential: <code>tn1e</code>, <code>tn1f</code>, <code>tn1g</code>. Someone could try every code and find private document links or reset links. Keep uniqueness but hide the order. Scramble each ID with a reversible keyed shuffle before encoding it, or choose seven random characters and let a unique database key reject a rare duplicate."],"callout":"Hard-to-guess codes slow scraping, but they are not access control. Private content still needs authorization."},{"title":"Cache popular redirects","body":["A small share of links gets most of the clicks, and a link rarely changes after creation. Add an in-memory cache from <code>code → long_url</code>. About 90% of redirects can finish in the cache within a millisecond; the database sees only misses. With immutable links there is little to invalidate, so a time limit (TTL) and least-recently-used eviction can be enough."],"callout":"Cache “no such code” too. Bots request random codes; otherwise every invalid code triggers a database lookup."},{"title":"Choose a redirect status","body":["A <code>301 Moved Permanently</code> tells the browser to remember the destination. Later clicks may not reach your service, so you lose click counts and control over the link. A <code>302 Found</code> is temporary, so the browser comes back to your service. That creates more requests but lets you collect analytics, change the destination, or expire the link. Most shorteners use 302 and let the cache handle the load."],"callout":"A status code changes who can cache the result and how long it can be reused."},{"title":"Spread billions of links across storage","body":["Six billion rows may be too much for one machine. Each query looks up one code; it does not need joins, range scans, or multi-row transactions. A <b>key–value store</b>, such as DynamoDB or Cassandra, fits that access pattern. Partition by a hash of the code to spread traffic, and keep three copies of each partition in separate zones."],"callout":"Random-looking codes spread well when hashed. Partitioning by creation date would send most new traffic to “today.”"},{"title":"Count clicks asynchronously","body":["Updating the link row on every redirect—<code>UPDATE links SET clicks = clicks + 1</code>—turns 4,000 reads per second into 4,000 writes, concentrated on popular links. Instead, put a small click event on a stream and return the redirect right away. A consumer adds events into per-link counts every few seconds."],"callout":"The redirect is the product; the click count is supporting data. Keep that extra work off the critical path."},{"title":"Protect the service from spam","body":["A short link hides its destination, which makes it useful for phishing. A script creates 5,000 links each minute to fake bank pages. If the domain lands on blocklists, every customer’s links may stop working. Limit link creation by account and IP, check each destination against phishing and malware lists at creation and later, and expire abandoned links."],"callout":"Operations are part of the design. For many shorteners, abuse is a more likely first incident than heavy traffic."},{"title":"Recap the finished design","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Choice</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Codes</td><td>Scrambled counter in base 62</td><td>Unique without collision checks</td></tr><tr><td>Reads</td><td>Cache and 302</td><td>About 100 reads per write</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>Hash-partitioned key–value store</td><td>One-key lookups; 3 TB</td></tr><tr><td>Clicks</td><td>Events on a stream</td><td>Not on the redirect path</td></tr><tr><td>Abuse</td><td>Limits, checks, expiry</td><td>Protect the domain</td></tr></tbody></table>The process matters more than the exact choices: requirements → estimates → simplest design → find the limit → fix it → repeat."],"callout":"Next: <b>Design a News Feed</b> — where the hard question is when to do the work."}]},"design-news-feed":{"summary":"Design a fast home feed and choose when to gather posts for each reader.","goals":["Estimate feed reads and post writes","Compare building a feed on read with building it on write","Understand why celebrities need a hybrid approach","Choose what to store in each feed","Use cursors and rebuild derived feed data"],"steps":[{"title":"Start with the home-feed requirements","body":["Design a social app home screen that shows recent posts from accounts a person follows, newest first. Opening the feed should feel instant—under 200 ms. New posts should reach followers within a few seconds, but not necessarily everyone at once. A delay of five seconds is acceptable, so the feed can be <b>eventually consistent</b>."],"callout":"It helps to say what does not need to be immediate or exact. That flexibility can make a large system practical."},{"title":"Estimate the workload","body":["Suppose <b>200 million</b> people use the app each day and open their feed about ten times. That is 2 billion feed loads daily, or about 23,000 per second on average. They write 50 million posts daily, about 600 per second. Feed reads are about <b>40 times</b> more common than posts. A typical person follows 200 accounts."],"callout":"Two numbers shape the design: there are 40 feed reads per post, and each feed may draw posts from 200 accounts."},{"title":"First design: build the feed when asked","body":["Store posts in a table indexed by <code>(author_id, created_at)</code> and split the data by author. To build Alice’s feed, find the 200 accounts she follows, fetch their newest posts, merge them by time, and return the newest 20. This is <span class=\"term\">fan-out on read</span>, or the pull model. Posting requires only one insert, and the new post is available to followers immediately when they request the feed."],"callout":"Nothing is precomputed, so there is no stale feed to repair. This is a good design for a small app."},{"title":"Too much work happens during a read","body":["Each feed load asks up to 200 authors across many shards and waits for the slowest response. At 23,000 feed loads per second, 200 lookups each means <b>4.6 million</b> lookups per second. Most recompute a feed that the same person saw a minute ago. The system puts expensive work on the read path, which runs 40 times more often than posts are written."],"callout":"When reads far outnumber writes, consider doing more of the work when a post is written."},{"title":"Build each feed when a post is written","body":["Keep a ready-made, newest-first list for every user. When Ana posts, a <b>fan-out worker</b> finds her followers and adds the post to their lists. This is <span class=\"term\">fan-out on write</span>, or the push model. Reading a feed becomes one list lookup. A queue runs the fan-out in the background, so Ana gets an immediate response and followers see the post a second or two later."],"callout":"At 600 posts per second and about 200 followers per post, this creates about 120,000 small cache writes each second—much less work than 4.6 million lookups."},{"title":"Store post IDs, not full posts","body":["Each feed list stores only <b>post IDs</b>, a few bytes each, and keeps only the newest 800 or so. When a feed is read, the API fetches the post records in one batch. This is sometimes called <b>hydration</b>. Keeping one copy of each post means edits and deletes happen once. If a post was deleted, the API skips it while building the response."],"callout":"Two hundred million lists × 800 IDs × 8 bytes is about 1.3 TB of memory. A large cache cluster can hold that; storing full posts in each feed could cost about 100 times more."},{"title":"One celebrity has 50 million followers","body":["A pop star with <b>50 million</b> followers posts. That one post creates 50 million cache writes. At 100,000 writes per second, delivery takes more than eight minutes. The job queue backs up, so everyone else’s posts wait too. This is a hot-key problem: one account creates far more work than the average."],"callout":"The typical account has 200 followers, but a few thousand accounts have millions—and they may post during the busiest periods."},{"title":"Use push for most users, pull for celebrities","body":["Use different methods for different account sizes. Push posts from ordinary users to their followers’ feeds. Do not fan out posts from accounts above a threshold, such as 100,000 followers. When Alice opens her feed, read her ready-made list and also fetch the newest posts from the few celebrities she follows. Merge those results. A few extra reads are cheaper than 50 million writes per post."],"callout":"Real systems often combine push and pull because a small share of accounts have very different traffic."},{"title":"Skip feeds for people who are inactive","body":["Perhaps only a third of Ana’s followers opened the app this month. Filling the other lists uses memory for feeds nobody reads. Fan out only to followers active in the last 30 days and let other lists expire. When an inactive user returns, build their feed once using the pull method, cache it, then resume pushing new posts."],"callout":"The pull path remains useful for creating, repairing, and rebuilding feeds. Push is an optimization on top of it."},{"title":"Scroll with a cursor","body":["Alice asks for more posts. “Page 2” could mean skip 20 items and return the next 20. But three new posts arrived at the top, so this may show Alice three posts twice. A <span class=\"term\">cursor</span> is tied to a specific post: “return 20 posts older than post 9017.” New posts cannot shift that position."],"callout":"Time-ordered IDs make the cursor simple: “older than” can mean “ID less than.”"},{"title":"Rebuild a wiped feed cache safely","body":["A bad deployment clears the feed cache. No original posts are lost because posts and follows are the <b>source of truth</b>. Feed lists are <b>derived data</b> and can be rebuilt. The risk is a rush of millions of pull-based rebuilds at once. Rebuild only when a user opens the app, limit how many rebuilds run at once, and serve a smaller feed until the cache is warm."],"callout":"Know which data is the source of truth and which is a convenience. Derived data is safe to rebuild only if the original data is protected."},{"title":"Recap the finished design","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Choice</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Build feeds</td><td>Push on write</td><td>40 reads per post</td></tr><tr><td>Celebrities</td><td>Pull and merge at read time</td><td>50 million writes per post</td></tr><tr><td>Feed entries</td><td>Post IDs, limited to recent items</td><td>Keep one copy of each post</td></tr><tr><td>Inactive users</td><td>Skip fan-out; rebuild on return</td><td>Unused feeds take memory</td></tr><tr><td>Scrolling</td><td>Cursors</td><td>New posts shift numbered pages</td></tr></tbody></table>A ranked feed adds a scoring step before returning posts. The same underlying data flow still applies."],"callout":"Next: <b>Design a Chat System</b> — where messages need to arrive reliably and in order."}]},"design-chat":{"summary":"Design chat for millions of open connections, durable messages, offline delivery, and ordered conversation history.","goals":["Estimate open connections as well as message volume","Route messages between connection gateways","Store messages before delivering them","Support offline sync and remove duplicates","Handle group chats, presence, and gateway failures"],"steps":[{"title":"Start with the chat requirements","body":["Design one-to-one and small-group chat, like WhatsApp or Messenger. Messages should arrive quickly when the other person is online, wait safely when they are offline, and appear on all of their devices. The main promise is that no message is lost, order is kept, and a message is shown once. When both people are online, delivery should take under 200 ms."],"callout":"A feed can be late and approximate. Chat must keep messages complete and ordered. The same building blocks can support different requirements."},{"title":"Estimate messages and connections","body":["Suppose <b>50 million</b> people use the app each day and send 40 messages each. That is 2 billion messages daily, about 23,000 per second and more at peak. At about 200 bytes each, that is 400 GB per day, or about 150 TB per year. Chat also keeps connections open: at peak, about <b>20 million</b> devices are connected."],"callout":"One well-tuned server may hold a few hundred thousand mostly idle connections. Twenty million connections could need about 100 servers just to keep sockets open."},{"title":"Begin with two WebSockets","body":["Polling would waste requests and add delay. Instead, each phone opens a <span class=\"term\">WebSocket</span>, a long-lived connection the server can use to send a message at any time. With one server, Alice and Bob both connect to it. When Alice sends a message, the server finds Bob’s socket in its memory and writes to it."],"callout":"Unlike a stateless server, this server is <b>stateful</b>: Bob’s connection exists on one particular machine. That fact shapes the design."},{"title":"Many gateways: where is Bob?","body":["Twenty million open connections need about 100 servers. Let <b>gateways</b> hold connections, and put the chat logic in a separate stateless chat service. Alice connects to gateway 1 and Bob to gateway 2. Alice’s message reaches the chat service, but it does not know which gateway holds Bob’s socket."],"callout":"Keep gateways focused on connections. Put frequently changing chat logic behind them, so it can be deployed without dropping all open sockets."},{"title":"Track each user’s gateway","body":["When a device connects, its gateway writes an entry in a fast store: <code>bob → gateway 2</code>. The entry has a short <span class=\"term\">TTL</span> and heartbeats renew it while the connection is alive. The chat service looks up Bob, sends the message to his gateway, and that gateway writes it to his socket. This is service discovery for individual users."],"callout":"If Bob’s entry is missing or stale, treat him as offline. The message needs a safe place to wait until he reconnects."},{"title":"Store before delivering","body":["So far, a message exists only in memory while it travels. If a server crashes during delivery, it could disappear. Use the rule <b>store first, then deliver</b>. The chat service writes the message to durable storage and assigns the conversation’s next <b>sequence number</b>. Only after the write succeeds does it tell Alice “sent.” The server—not the phones’ clocks—sets the order."],"callout":"Partition storage by conversation and sort by sequence number. A write-heavy store such as Cassandra or HBase can fit this access pattern."},{"title":"Let offline users sync later","body":["Bob loses signal in a tunnel, and his gateway entry expires. The message is already stored. A push notification through Apple’s or Google’s service can wake his phone. When Bob reconnects, his app says it has conversation 88 through message #41, and the server returns everything after that. This <b>cursor sync</b> works after a short gap, a week offline, or on a new device."],"callout":"Live delivery is an optimization. The reliable process is “store, then sync”; an open socket only makes syncing happen sooner."},{"title":"Show a retried message only once","body":["Alice taps send in a lift. The server stores the message, but the “sent” reply is lost. Alice’s app cannot tell whether it worked, so it retries. Give each message a unique ID on the device before sending and reuse it for every retry. The server recognizes IDs it has already stored and acknowledges them again. Receiving apps also discard duplicate IDs."],"callout":"At-least-once delivery plus receivers that safely ignore duplicates can make the message appear once in the app. A ✓ means stored by the server; ✓✓ means received by the device."},{"title":"Store group messages once, then fan out","body":["Store a group message once in the group conversation. Then find the members and each online member’s gateway, and send a copy to each device. This is fan-out on write and works for groups up to a few hundred people. A channel with 100,000 members creates too many deliveries at once; apps can fetch new messages when someone opens the channel instead."],"callout":"Offline members do not need an immediate push. They sync from their own cursor when they return."},{"title":"Treat presence as temporary","body":["Presence shows whether someone is “online” or “typing…”. When 20 million people connect and disconnect, updating every contact can create a storm of small messages. Treat presence as <b>ephemeral</b>: derive “online” from connection heartbeats, send updates only to people viewing that contact list, and do not store “typing…” at all. If a presence update is missed, the next heartbeat corrects it."],"callout":"Messages are durable and ordered. Presence is best-effort and may be seconds old. A system can use different consistency levels for different features."},{"title":"Recover from a gateway failure","body":["Gateway 2 crashes and 200,000 sockets disconnect. Messages remain safe because they were stored first. But 200,000 apps may try to reconnect at once and overload another gateway. Have apps reconnect with <b>exponential backoff and jitter</b>. The load balancer spreads them across healthy gateways, each app registers its new location, and messages sync from its cursor."],"callout":"Plan for the reconnect rush when stateful servers fail. Before a deployment, drain gateways gently instead of disconnecting everyone at once."},{"title":"Recap the finished design","body":["<table class=\"compare\"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Choice</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Transport</td><td>WebSockets on gateways</td><td>Server push; 20 million connections</td></tr><tr><td>Routing</td><td>Registry maps user to gateway</td><td>Connections are stateful</td></tr><tr><td>Durability</td><td>Store before delivery</td><td>Do not lose messages</td></tr><tr><td>Order and duplicates</td><td>Server sequence and device IDs</td><td>Clocks drift; retries happen</td></tr><tr><td>Offline</td><td>Push and cursor sync</td><td>One recovery path for any gap</td></tr></tbody></table>End-to-end encryption changes what the server can read, not the message flow. The server stores and forwards encrypted text."],"callout":"That completes the course. You now have the building blocks and three full system designs. Try sketching how you would build a product you use every day."}]}};
